OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026", "title": "The Partition Principle does not imply Choice", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/partition-principle-without-choice.pdf", "family": "244", "family_title": "The Partition Principle does not imply Choice", "abstract_html": "We prove that the Partition Principle does not imply the Axiom of Choice: if ZF is consistent, then so is ZF with the Partition Principle, Choice for ordinal-indexed families, and the negation of the Axiom of Choice. Separately, over every countable transitive model of ZFC, we construct a transitive symmetric model of this theory with the same ordinals and no new countable sequences of ground elements.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">The Partition Principle is\n  \\[\n  \\mathrm{PP}:\\quad\n  \\forall X\\,\\forall Y\\,\n  \\bigl[(\\exists f:X\\twoheadrightarrow Y)\n  \\Longrightarrow(\\exists j:Y\\hookrightarrow X)\\bigr].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathrm{AC}_{\\mathrm{WO}}\\) is Choice for wellorderable families: for every ordinal \\(\\eta\\) and every family \\((X_\\xi)_{\\xi&lt;\\eta}\\) of nonempty sets, there is a function \\(c\\) satisfying \\(c(\\xi)\\in X_\\xi\\) for every \\(\\xi&lt;\\eta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\) is classical first-order set theory with pure sets, Foundation, and the full Separation and Replacement schemata.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{Con}(\\mathrm{ZF})\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Con}\n\\bigl(\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{PP}+\\mathrm{AC}_{\\mathrm{WO}}+\\neg\\mathrm{AC}\\bigr).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume \\(\\operatorname{Con}(\\mathrm{ZF})\\). Proposition 14 gives\n\\(\\operatorname{Con}(\\mathrm{ZFC}+V=L)\\). Completeness and downward Löwenheim–Skolem give a countable model \\(M\\) of this theory; external transitivity or well-foundedness is not asserted.</p><p>Carry out the construction of Proposition 13 internally in \\(M\\). It supplies a nonempty set symmetric system satisfying the internal forcing assertion (8.1).</p><p>By Proposition 15, choose an external \\(M\\)-generic filter and form the symmetric quotient \\(W\\) of internal names. The truth lemma gives full \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\), and transfers (8.1), so\n\\[\nW\\models\n\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{PP}+\\neg\\mathrm{AC}+\\mathrm{AC}_{\\mathrm{WO}}.\n\\]\nSoundness yields the required consistency statement.</p><p>Equivalently, a finite contradiction proof from the target theory uses finitely many \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\) instances. Translate its lines into symmetric forcing. Logical forcing rules preserve inferences; Lemma 15.1 supplies its \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\) axioms; (8.1) supplies the three additional sentences. A nonempty poset would then force a contradiction, contrary to atomic forcing laws. This would yield a contradiction proof in \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}+V=L\\), and Proposition 14 translates it into one in \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\). No stronger model-existence assumption is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumption.", "statement": "<p>Assumption.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(V\\) is a countable transitive model of \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a set forcing \\(\\mathbb Q\\in V\\), a \\(V\\)-generic filter \\(G\\), and a transitive symmetric submodel \\(W\\) of \\(V[G]\\) such that\n\\[\nV\\subseteq W\\subseteq V[G],\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Ord}^{W}=\\operatorname{Ord}^{V},\n\\qquad\nW\\models\n\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{PP}+\\mathrm{AC}_{\\mathrm{WO}}+\\neg\\mathrm{AC}.\n\\]\nEvery countable sequence of elements of \\(V\\) that belongs to \\(W\\) already belongs to \\(V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Work in a \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}\\) metatheory, and let \\(V\\) be the given countable transitive model. Carry out Proposition 13 inside \\(V\\), with\n\\[\n\\kappa=\\omega_1^V,\\qquad\n\\mu=((2^{\\aleph_0})^+)^V,\\qquad\nI=(\\mu^+)^V.\n\\]</p><p>Externally enumerate the dense subsets of the constructed product forcing that belong to \\(V\\), and choose a descending sequence meeting them. Its upward-generated filter \\(G\\) is \\(V\\)-generic.</p><p>Because \\(V\\) is transitive, genuine well-founded recursion on names is available. Define\n\\[\nW=\\{\\tau^G:\\tau\\in\\mathrm{HS}^V\\}.\n\\]\nProposition 4 gives transitive models\n\\[\nV\\subseteq W\\subseteq V[G]\n\\]\nwith the same ordinals and with \\(W\\models\\mathrm{ZF}\\). Proposition 13 gives \\(\\mathrm{PP}\\), \\(\\mathrm{AC}_{\\mathrm{WO}}\\), and \\(\\neg\\mathrm{AC}\\) in that same \\(W\\), together with preservation of countable sequences from every fixed ground set.</p><p>For a countable sequence \\(f\\in W\\) of elements of \\(V\\), its rank in \\(V[G]\\) is bounded by a ground ordinal \\(\\alpha\\), since forcing preserves ordinals. Ground ranks are absolute between these transitive models, so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ran}(f)\\subseteq(V_\\alpha)^V.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 13 to this ground set to conclude \\(f\\in V\\).</p><p>This deduction retains the given transitive ground. The ordinary consistency proof of Theorem 1 permits an externally ill-founded ground and does not assert existence of a transitive model.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Remark 7.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\leq^{*}Y\\) means that \\(X=\\varnothing\\) or there is a surjection \\(Y\\twoheadrightarrow X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(X&lt;Y\\) means that there is an injection \\(X\\hookrightarrow Y\\) but no bijection \\(X\\to Y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The dual Cantor–Schröder–Bernstein principle \\(\\mathrm{CSB}^{*}\\) asserts that\n  \\[\n  X\\leq^{*}Y\\ \\land\\ Y\\leq^{*}X\n  \\Longrightarrow X\\cong Y.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The Weak Partition Principle \\(\\mathrm{WPP}\\) asserts that\n  \\[\n  X\\leq^{*}Y\\Longrightarrow\\neg(Y&lt;X).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\),\n\\[\n\\mathrm{PP}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{CSB}^{*}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{WPP}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Con}(\\mathrm{ZF})\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Con}\\bigl(\n\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{PP}+\\mathrm{AC}_{\\mathrm{WO}}\n+\\mathrm{CSB}^{*}+\\mathrm{WPP}+\\neg\\mathrm{AC}\n\\bigr).\n\\]\nAssuming \\(\\operatorname{Con}(\\mathrm{ZF})\\), neither\n\\(\\mathrm{CSB}^{*}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}\\) nor\n\\(\\mathrm{WPP}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}\\) is provable in \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\).\nThe model \\(W\\) of Theorem 2 also satisfies \\(\\mathrm{CSB}^{*}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{WPP}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Work first in \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\).</p><p>If \\(X\\leq^{*}Y\\), then \\(X\\hookrightarrow Y\\) follows from \\(\\mathrm{PP}\\), using the empty injection when \\(X=\\varnothing\\). Thus mutual \\(\\leq^{*}\\) gives injections in both directions, and ordinary Cantor–Schröder–Bernstein gives \\(X\\cong Y\\). This proves\n\\[\n\\mathrm{PP}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{CSB}^{*}.\n\\]</p><p>Suppose \\(\\mathrm{CSB}^{*}\\), \\(X\\leq^{*}Y\\), and \\(Y&lt;X\\). The injection \\(Y\\hookrightarrow X\\) gives \\(Y\\leq^{*}X\\): for nonempty \\(Y\\), invert it on its image and extend by one fixed element of \\(Y\\); for empty \\(Y\\), use the definition. Dual Cantor–Schröder–Bernstein gives \\(X\\cong Y\\), contradicting \\(Y&lt;X\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CSB}^{*}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{WPP}.\n\\]\nIn a mutual-\\(\\leq^{*}\\) hypothesis, if either set is empty then so is the other.</p><p>These are internal \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\) implications. Apply them to the model supplied by Theorem 1 to obtain the stated consistency result and the failures of provable implication to Choice. Apply them to the model supplied by Theorem 2 to obtain the transitive-model conclusion. Ordinal-indexed choice remains true in these models but is not needed for the implication chain.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Theorem 2.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Work in \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}\\), and fix an infinite cardinal \\(\\mu\\geq\\aleph_1\\) satisfying \\(\\mu^{\\aleph_0}=\\mu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a monoid \\(M\\) with identity \\(1\\), write\n  \\[\n  x\\sqsubseteq y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n  (\\exists z\\in M)\\,xz=y,\n  \\qquad\n  \\downarrow a=\\{x\\in M:x\\sqsubseteq a\\}.\n  \\]\n  Write \\(x\\sqsubset y\\) when \\(x\\sqsubseteq y\\) and \\(x\\neq y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A monoid is conical if \\(xy=1\\) implies \\(x=y=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A conditional join \\(x\\vee y\\) is required exactly when \\(xM\\cap yM\\neq\\varnothing\\), and satisfies\n  \\[\n  xM\\cap yM=(x\\vee y)M.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">An ideal in \\(\\downarrow a\\) is a nonempty downward closed subset closed under finite nonempty joins.</p><p class=\"li\">An inclusion \\(B\\subseteq M\\) is conservative if it preserves and reflects comparisons, preserves their right factors and existing joins, and preserves the absence of a common right multiple. In particular, \\(x,y\\in B\\) and \\(xz=y\\) in \\(M\\) imply \\(z\\in B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Height and cost functions satisfy\n  \\[\n  0&lt;\\rho(x)&lt;1,\\qquad 0&lt;\\epsilon_x\\leq1,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\rho(y)-\\rho(x)&gt;\n  \\sup\\{\\epsilon_d:d\\sqsubseteq y,\\ d\\not\\sqsubseteq x\\}\n  \\qquad(x\\sqsubset y).\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]\n  An empty cost supremum is \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A finite overlap prescription on copies \\(\\{i\\}\\times M\\) specifies, for each distinct pair, either no overlap or elements \\(a_{ij},a_{ji}\\in M\\) and identifications\n  \\[\n  (i,a_{ij}x)\\sim(j,a_{ji}x)\\qquad(x\\in M).\n  \\]\n  It is valid if these pair identifications, together with equality, already form an equivalence relation and do not identify distinct elements within one copy.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a monoid \\(T\\) of cardinality \\(\\mu\\), with a distinguished \\(e\\neq1\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(T\\) is left cancellative and conical, has conditional joins, and has height and cost functions satisfying (2.1).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every valid finite overlap prescription on copies of \\(T\\) is realized by left translations \\(x\\mapsto l_i x\\) in \\(T\\), with exactly the prescribed identifications.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For \\(a,b\\in T\\), \\(b\\neq1\\), and every ideal \\(U\\subseteq\\downarrow a\\), there are \\(\\mu\\) distinct elements \\(m\\) satisfying\n   \\[\n   mb=ae,\\qquad m\\not\\sqsubseteq a,\\qquad\n   \\downarrow m\\cap\\downarrow a=U,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n\\vee m=ae\n   \\qquad(n\\sqsubseteq a,\\ n\\notin U).\n   \\tag{2.2}\n   \\]\n   The cones of distinct elements in this family intersect exactly in \\(aeT\\). Moreover,\n   \\[\n   |T\\setminus eT|=\\mu.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Every countable subset of \\(T\\) is contained in a countable submonoid \\(T_0\\) from which a continuous conservative tower ends at \\(T\\). At each successor step either:</p><p class=\"li l2\">no new element divides an old element; or</p><p class=\"li l2\">every new element \\(w\\) with an old right multiple has a least old right multiple \\(g(w)\\), and\n     \\[\n     d\\text{ old},\\quad g(w)\\sqsubseteq wd\n     \\Longrightarrow wd\\text{ old}.\n     \\tag{2.3}\n     \\]\n   Here “old” refers to the base of that successor step; the comparisons may be tested in \\(T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Saturating the geometric requirements. Start with the free monoid on \\(e\\). For \\(n&lt;\\omega\\), put\n\\[\n\\rho(e^n)=1-2^{-n-1},\\qquad\n\\epsilon_{e^n}=2^{-n-3}.\n\\]\nThese satisfy (2.1).</p><p>Perform \\(\\omega_1\\) rounds. At the beginning of a round, schedule every available probe datum \\((a,b,(n_i))\\), using the fixed \\(e\\), and every finite valid merger. For each probe datum schedule \\(\\mu\\) successive insertions. Execute the wellordered list using Lemmas 1.2–1.4 and take unions at limits. Lemma 1.5 keeps scheduled mergers valid.</p><p>A monoid of size at most \\(\\mu\\) has at most \\(\\mu^{\\aleph_0}=\\mu\\) countable paths and at most \\(\\mu\\) finite prescriptions. A probe uses a quotient of \\(M\\times\\omega\\) and finite strings; a merger uses finitely many copies and finite strings. Every operation preserves size at most \\(\\mu\\), and every round has at most \\(\\mu\\) steps. The union \\(T\\) of \\(\\omega_1\\) rounds has size at most \\(\\mu\\). Old height and cost values remain fixed, and old missed-cost suprema never increase. The final union satisfies (2.1) and the algebraic requirements.</p><p>For an ideal \\(U\\subseteq\\downarrow_T a\\), Lemma 1.1 gives a cofinal path starting at \\(1\\). Its elements, \\(a,b\\), and all transition and terminal quotients occur before one round, since the set is countable and the rounds have cofinality \\(\\omega_1\\). Quotient conservativity also places each quotient in a stage containing its endpoints. The corresponding requirement is scheduled. Its final downward closure is \\(U\\), so Lemma 1.6 gives (2.2) for the \\(\\mu\\) insertions.</p><p>For an earlier and a later insertion with the same datum, the earlier probe is old but does not divide \\(a\\), so it is not an old divisor of the new probe. Joining through the new probe’s gate gives \\(ae\\), and the join persists.</p><p>Every final finite overlap prescription has parameters in a stage. Validity reflects to that stage by Lemma 1.5, and the schedule realizes it later. Exactness persists: any later equality \\(l_i x=l_j y\\) factors through the preserved join of the roots and its prescribed quotients, so later right factors add only the stipulated identifications.</p><p>For \\(a=1\\), \\(b=e\\), and \\(U=\\{1\\}\\), the probes have no old divisor \\(e\\). Thus there are \\(\\mu\\) elements of \\(T\\setminus eT\\), proving both cardinality assertions.</p><p>Extracting a countable initial base. Regard the schedule as a continuous tower \\((M_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\leq\\lambda}\\) of individual operations. Record parameters by finite words in earlier generators. Every element has such a word, and every equality has a finite derivation from the relations; this does not give finite support to an entire countable path.</p><p>Choose a sufficiently large regular \\(\\Theta\\) and a countable elementary submodel \\(N\\prec H_\\Theta\\) containing the tower, its presentations, and an enumeration of the prescribed countable subset of \\(T\\). Countable objects in \\(N\\), including parameter paths for operations indexed in \\(N\\), have all their entries in \\(N\\).</p><p>Perform only the operations indexed in \\(N\\), in their original order. Let \\(S_\\alpha\\) be the selected subconstruction below the cut \\(\\alpha\\). At cuts \\(\\alpha\\in N\\),\n\\[\nS_\\alpha=M_\\alpha\\cap N,\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\nwith a conservative inclusion into \\(M_\\alpha\\).</p><p>For the induction, comparisons, quotients, and joins in an elementary intersection are reflected by elementarity, and a nonjoin remains a nonjoin. Before a selected operation, every parameter is present: its finite term code belongs to \\(N\\), and all entries of a parameter path in \\(N\\) belong to \\(N\\). Lemma 1.5 makes the operation legal and embeds its result. Conversely, each element of \\(M_\\alpha\\cap N\\) has by elementarity a finite term code in \\(N\\); all its generator indices belong to \\(N\\) and precede \\(\\alpha\\), so the selected construction produces it.</p><p>At a cut \\(\\alpha\\notin N\\), fix finitely many words in a comparison or join test. If selected operation indices occur, let \\(\\gamma\\) be the greatest and put \\(\\beta=\\gamma+1\\); otherwise put \\(\\beta=0\\). Then \\(\\beta\\in N\\), \\(\\beta&lt;\\alpha\\), and the words belong to \\(S_\\beta=M_\\beta\\cap N\\). Conservativity of \\(M_\\beta\\subseteq M_\\alpha\\) puts every required quotient or conditional join in \\(M_\\beta\\), and elementarity puts it in \\(N\\), hence in \\(S_\\beta\\). Nonjoins are also preserved. Unions introduce no additional finite relation.</p><p>The selected union \\(T_0\\) is countable: the initial monoid is countable, there are countably many selected operations, and each operation over a countable base adds countably many elements. Finite term codes ensure that \\(T_0\\) contains the prescribed sequence.</p><p>Reinserting omitted operations. Append omitted operations in their original order. Before an omitted step \\(\\alpha\\), the current presentation contains the original relations below \\(\\alpha\\) and all selected relations. It can alternatively be realized by starting with \\(M_\\alpha\\) and performing the selected steps of index at least \\(\\alpha\\). The conservative inclusion \\(S_\\alpha\\subseteq M_\\alpha\\) and repeated conservative simulation make those steps legal. This alternative realization conservatively contains \\(M_\\alpha\\) and has exactly the current generators and relations.</p><p>Therefore the original operation at \\(\\alpha\\) is legal over the current base by Lemma 1.5. Induction validates all appended operations. Limits are unions, and the final presentation is the original presentation of \\(T\\).</p><p>Each reordered step is a merger, which has no new old divisors, or a probe extension, which has the least-old-multiple rule (2.3). Conservativity permits comparisons to be tested in \\(T\\). This proves the countable-base assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) has conditional joins and functions \\(\\rho,\\epsilon\\) satisfying (2.1).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(U\\) is an ideal in \\(\\downarrow a\\), for some \\(a\\in M\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The ideal \\(U\\) has a nondecreasing cofinal sequence of length \\(\\omega\\), with repetitions allowed and with first term \\(1\\).</p><p>The same assertion holds for ideals in any \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}\\) extension in which the monoid, its order, and its height and cost functions retain their meanings.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose elements of \\(U\\) whose heights approach \\(\\sup\\rho[U]\\), and replace successive initial segments by their finite joins. This gives a nondecreasing sequence \\((n_i)\\) in \\(U\\) whose heights approach the same supremum.</p><p>If \\(d\\in U\\) divides none of the \\(n_i\\), then\n\\[\n\\rho(n_i\\vee d)-\\rho(n_i)&gt;\\epsilon_d\n\\]\nfor every \\(i\\), contradicting convergence to the supremum. Prepending \\(1\\) makes \\(n_0=1\\).</p><p>The same proof applies in the stated extension; its choices are made in that \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}\\) universe.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is left cancellative and conical and has conditional joins.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(a,b,e\\in M\\), with \\(b,e\\neq1\\), and a nondecreasing path\n  \\[\n  1=n_0\\sqsubseteq n_1\\sqsubseteq\\cdots\\sqsubseteq a,\n  \\qquad\n  n_i d_i=n_{i+1},\\qquad n_i c_i=a.\n  \\tag{2.4}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M'\\) be the monoid presented over \\(M\\) by new generators \\(p_i\\), \\(i&lt;\\omega\\), and relations\n  \\[\n  p_i=d_i p_{i+1},\\qquad p_i b=c_i e.\n  \\tag{2.5}\n  \\]\n  Put \\(m=p_0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The monoid \\(M'\\) is left cancellative and conical, has conditional joins, and conservatively contains \\(M\\). Every new element with an old right multiple has a least old right multiple, and (2.3) holds at this extension. Furthermore,\n\\[\nmb=ae,\\qquad m\\not\\sqsubseteq a,\\qquad\n\\downarrow m\\cap\\downarrow a=\\bigcup_{i&lt;\\omega}\\downarrow n_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nx\\vee m=ae\n\\qquad(x\\sqsubseteq a,\\ x\\not\\sqsubseteq m),\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nwhere the downsets and joins are computed in \\(M'\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Left cancellation in \\(M\\) gives \\(d_i c_{i+1}=c_i\\).</p><p>Normal forms. Let \\(S\\) be the direct limit of the sets \\(M\\) under the maps \\(x\\mapsto xd_i\\). Write \\([x,i]\\) for the image of \\(x\\) at stage \\(i\\). Equality means equality at a finite common stage. The transition maps need not be injective, since right cancellation has not been assumed. Nevertheless, the left \\(M\\)-action on \\(S\\) is injective for each multiplier: equality after multiplication by \\(u\\in M\\) becomes \\(uX=uY\\) at a common stage, and left cancellation gives \\(X=Y\\).</p><p>There are well-defined equivariant maps\n\\[\nk([x,i])=xc_i,\\qquad h(s)=k(s)e.\n\\]\nBefore imposing the second relations in (2.5), normal forms are strings\n\\[\ns_1\\cdots s_j y\\qquad(s_i\\in S,\\ y\\in M),\n\\]\nincluding \\(j=0\\). The letter \\([x,i]\\) represents \\(xp_i\\). Old factors between new generators are absorbed into the following letter, and the transition relations are precisely equality in \\(S\\). Multiplication concatenates strings, using the left action to absorb the terminal old factor of the first string.</p><p>The remaining reductions are\n\\[\ns(bt)\\longmapsto h(s)t,\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\nwhere \\(t\\) is a letter of \\(S\\) or a terminal old element. The quotient \\(t\\) is unique because the left \\(b\\)-action is injective. Each reduction removes one new letter. The only overlapping reductions occur in \\(s(bt)(bu)\\), with the final factor possibly terminal. Both orders of reduction agree by\n\\[\nh(bt)=bh(t),\\qquad h(h(s)t)=h(s)h(t).\n\\]\nDisjoint reductions commute. Termination and these local diamonds give unique irreducibles by Newman’s lemma: besides old elements, they are strings whose first letter is arbitrary and whose later letters and terminal factor are not left-divisible by \\(b\\).</p><p>Old left multipliers act injectively. Left multiplication by a letter \\(s\\) either prepends \\(s\\), when the input is not a \\(b\\)-multiple, or sends \\(bZ\\) to \\(h(s)Z\\). Both cases are injective, and their ranges are disjoint because\n\\[\ns\\notin h(s)S.\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(s\\in xS\\) implies \\(x\\sqsubseteq k(s)\\), whereas \\(k(s)e\\not\\sqsubseteq k(s)\\) by conicality and left cancellation. Thus every left multiplication is injective. A new word can become old only by consuming its first letter, after which it is a multiple of \\(h(s)\\neq1\\). It cannot equal \\(1\\), proving conicality.</p><p>Old cones and first-letter blocks. For \\(s\\in S\\), let \\(B_s\\) consist of the irreducibles beginning with \\(s\\). For old \\(x\\),\n\\[\nxM'=xM\\cup\\bigcup_{s\\in xS}B_s.\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\nAt a common stage, \\(xS\\cap yS=(x\\vee y)S\\) if the old join exists, and the intersection is empty if the old cones are disjoint. Consequently old comparisons, their right factors, joins, and nonjoins are unchanged.</p><p>Exits and gates. Fix an irreducible \\(w=sY\\). The set \\(wM'\\setminus B_s\\) is either empty or one cone \\(g(w)M'\\) with old root \\(g(w)\\).</p><p>The first letter is consumed exactly when the suffix becomes a \\(b\\)-multiple. Calculate \\(YM'\\cap bM'\\). If \\(Y\\) is old, (2.9) gives its old join with \\(b\\), or an empty intersection. If \\(Y\\) is new with head \\(t\\), irreducibility gives \\(t\\notin bS\\), so no member of \\(B_t\\) is a \\(b\\)-multiple. Induction on the number of letters in the suffix says that all exits of \\(Y\\), if any, form \\(g(Y)M'\\). Its intersection with \\(bM'\\) is calculated by the old join \\(g(Y)\\vee b\\).</p><p>Thus the suffix intersection is empty or \\(zM'\\), for an old \\(z=bu\\) with old quotient \\(u\\). In the latter case,\n\\[\nwM'\\setminus B_s=g(w)M',\n\\qquad g(w)=h(s)u.\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\nThis cone cannot return to \\(B_s\\), since its new heads belong to \\(h(s)S\\), whereas (2.8) excludes \\(s\\). Every old multiple of \\(w\\) lies outside \\(B_s\\), so \\(g(w)\\) is its least old multiple.</p><p>Record the quotient \\(c(w)\\) defined by \\(wc(w)=g(w)\\). If \\(Y\\) is old, it is the old quotient of \\(z\\) by \\(Y\\). If \\(Y\\) is new, then\n\\[\nc(w)=c(Y)v,\\qquad g(Y)v=z.\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\nAll quotients used here are old except the already constructed \\(c(Y)\\).</p><p>Append an old \\(d\\) to \\(w\\). If the product leaves \\(B_s\\), consuming the first letter requires all intervening letters to have been consumed, and the result is entirely old. Since the gate cone is disjoint from \\(B_s\\), \\(g(w)\\sqsubseteq wd\\) implies such an exit. This proves (2.3).</p><p>Conditional joins. For forms with the same head, cancel the head and intersect suffix cones, inducting on the total number of new letters. For old \\(x\\) and new \\(w\\in B_s\\), either \\(s\\in xS\\), so \\(x\\sqsubseteq w\\), or every common multiple leaves \\(B_s\\), and the intersection is computed through \\(g(w)\\) and an old join.</p><p>For \\(w\\in B_s\\), \\(v\\in B_t\\), \\(s\\neq t\\), an intersection inside \\(B_s\\) requires \\(g(v)\\) to divide a member of \\(B_s\\). By (2.9), it then divides all of \\(B_s\\), so \\(v\\sqsubseteq w\\). The symmetric case is the same. Otherwise common multiples leave both blocks, and the intersection is calculated by the old join of the gates. Missing gates or old joins give an empty intersection.</p><p>The divisor profile. For a letter \\(s\\), the old divisors of every element of \\(B_s\\) form\n\\[\nP_s=\\{x\\in M:s\\in xS\\}\\subseteq\\downarrow k(s).\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\nCommon-stage representatives and old joins show that this is an ideal. For the head \\([1,0]\\) of \\(m=p_0\\), it is exactly \\(\\bigcup_i\\downarrow_M n_i\\), and the gate is \\(ae\\). The relations give \\(mb=ae\\). Since \\(ae\\not\\sqsubseteq a\\), no member of \\(B_{[1,0]}\\) divides \\(a\\), and in particular \\(m\\not\\sqsubseteq a\\).</p><p>If a new \\(x\\sqsubseteq a\\), it is outside that block and has an old gate \\(g(x)\\sqsubseteq a\\). If \\(x\\sqsubseteq m\\), the comparison leaves its block, so \\(g(x)\\sqsubseteq m\\); the old profile puts \\(g(x)\\) below some \\(n_i\\), and hence \\(x\\sqsubseteq n_i\\). Conversely \\(x\\sqsubseteq n_i\\) implies \\(x\\sqsubseteq m\\).</p><p>If \\(x\\not\\sqsubseteq m\\), its gate does not divide \\(m\\). The possibility \\(m\\sqsubseteq x\\) is excluded by \\(x\\sqsubseteq a\\). The gate calculation gives\n\\[\nx\\vee m=g(x)\\vee m=ae.\n\\]\nThe assertions for old \\(x\\) follow directly from block membership. This proves (2.6), including new divisors of \\(a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) has height and cost functions satisfying (2.1).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(M'\\) is the probe extension of Lemma 1.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The height and cost functions extend to \\(M'\\) so that (2.1) holds. For any old comparison, its missed-cost supremum is unchanged.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use \\(P_s\\) from (2.12), and put \\(A_s=\\sup\\rho[P_s]\\). Place the whole block \\(B_s\\) in a short height interval above its old divisors and below \\(\\rho(h(s))\\). The element \\(h(s)\\) is the gate of the bare letter \\(s\\); a longer word’s gate, when present, is a multiple of \\(h(s)\\).</p><p>There are positive \\(u_s,\\delta_s\\) such that\n\\[\nu_s-A_s&gt;\\delta_s,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho(h(s))-u_s-\\delta_s&gt;\n\\sup\\{\\epsilon_d:d\\sqsubseteq h(s),\\ d\\in M\\setminus P_s\\}.\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\nIndeed, put\n\\[\np=\\rho(k(s))-A_s\\geq0,\\qquad\nq=\\rho(h(s))-\\rho(k(s))&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor \\(d\\sqsubseteq k(s)\\) missing \\(P_s\\), every \\(x\\in P_s\\) misses \\(d\\), and the old margin gives\n\\[\n\\epsilon_d&lt;\\rho(k(s))-\\rho(x).\n\\]\nThe supremum of these costs is at most \\(p\\). The supremum for divisors of \\(h(s)\\) not dividing \\(k(s)\\) is strictly below \\(q\\). Thus the full supremum in (2.13) is strictly below \\(p+q=\\rho(h(s))-A_s\\), also when \\(p=0\\).</p><p>Define recursively on irreducibles\n\\[\n\\rho(sY)=u_s+\\delta_s\\rho(Y),\n\\qquad\n0&lt;\\epsilon_{sY}\\leq\\delta_s\\epsilon_Y,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\epsilon_{sY}\\leq\\epsilon_{g(sY)}\n\\quad\\text{whenever the gate exists}.\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]\nThese give the required ranges for heights and costs.</p><p>For two comparable elements in one block \\(B_s\\), a divisor of the upper element outside that block also divides the lower one. An old divisor belongs to \\(P_s\\); a new divisor with a different head enters by its old gate, which then divides every member of \\(B_s\\). Inside the block, cancellation removes \\(s\\), and induction applies with the factor \\(\\delta_s\\) in both height differences and cost bounds.</p><p>If an old \\(x\\sqsubset sY\\), then \\(x\\in P_s\\). A missed divisor outside \\(B_s\\) is old in \\(P_s\\), or has an old gate there of at least its cost. For such an old \\(d\\) missing \\(x\\), the join \\(x\\vee d\\) belongs to \\(P_s\\), and\n\\[\n\\epsilon_d&lt;\\rho(x\\vee d)-\\rho(x)\\leq A_s-\\rho(x).\n\\]\nTheir supremum is at most \\(A_s-\\rho(x)\\). Divisors inside \\(B_s\\) have cost at most \\(\\delta_s\\). Equation (2.13) makes \\(\\rho(sY)-\\rho(x)\\) strictly larger than both bounds.</p><p>If \\(sY\\sqsubset y\\) for old \\(y\\), then \\(h(s)\\sqsubseteq y\\). Every divisor of \\(y\\) missing \\(sY\\) is old outside \\(P_s\\), or has a gate there of at least its cost; a gate in \\(P_s\\) would put the divisor below all of \\(B_s\\). Costs from divisors of \\(h(s)\\) are bounded by (2.13). If \\(y\\neq h(s)\\), the remaining costs have supremum strictly below \\(\\rho(y)-\\rho(h(s))\\). The total gap \\(\\rho(y)-\\rho(sY)\\) exceeds both suprema.</p><p>Distinct comparable new blocks have an intervening old gate. The preceding cases combine transitively: if \\(x&lt;z&lt;y\\), a divisor of \\(y\\) missing \\(x\\) either divides \\(z\\) and misses \\(x\\), or misses \\(z\\); the sum of the two positive gaps strictly dominates both corresponding suprema.</p><p>Finally, for old \\(x&lt;y\\), a new divisor \\(d\\) of \\(y\\) missing \\(x\\) has an old gate \\(g(d)\\sqsubseteq y\\) missing \\(x\\), with \\(\\epsilon_d\\leq\\epsilon_{g(d)}\\). New costs therefore do not increase the old supremum, while every old divisor remains. This also proves persistence of margins at unions of conservative towers.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is left cancellative and conical, has conditional joins, and has height and cost functions satisfying (2.1).</p><p class=\"li\">A finite nonempty overlap prescription on copies of \\(M\\) is valid.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a conservative extension realizing the prescription by left translations. The extension is left cancellative and conical, has conditional joins, and admits extending height and cost functions satisfying (2.1). No new element divides an old element.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(D\\) be the prescribed quotient of the copies, and write \\(t_i x\\) for the image of \\(x\\) in copy \\(i\\). It is a right \\(M\\)-set, and each map \\(x\\mapsto t_i x\\) is injective. Define \\(z\\sqsubseteq w\\) in \\(D\\) by \\(zq=w\\) for some \\(q\\in M\\). Left cancellation and conicality inside a representing copy make this a partial order.</p><p>Its right cones have conditional joins. Within one copy use the old join. For elements represented in different copies, every common multiple lies in the specified overlap. In each copy join the element with the start of that overlap; transfer the two resulting elements into the overlap and join there. A missing join means the original cones are disjoint. Every common multiple passes through the preliminary joins, so this calculates exactly the intersection.</p><p>Adjoin generators \\(l_i\\) with relations \\(l_i a_{ij}=l_j a_{ji}\\). Normal forms are\n\\[\nxz_1\\cdots z_j\\qquad(x\\in M,\\ z_i\\in D),\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\nincluding \\(j=0\\). Relations act inside a new-generator/old-factor pair, exactly as in \\(D\\); they change neither the number of new letters nor the preceding old coefficient. This proves uniqueness.</p><p>Multiplication lets the leading old coefficient of the second word act on the last \\(D\\)-letter of the first, then appends the remaining letters. For fixed \\(z=t_i a\\), the map \\(y\\mapsto zy\\) is injective by cancellation in copy \\(i\\). Together with old left cancellation this proves left cancellation for all products. Letter count proves conicality and excludes new divisors of old elements.</p><p>For positive-length words, right multiplication fixes the prefix before the final letter, advances that letter in its \\(D\\)-cone, and possibly appends letters. Compare\n\\[\nxz_1\\cdots z_p,\\qquad yt_1\\cdots t_q,\\qquad p,q&gt;0.\n\\]\nA common multiple requires \\(x=y\\) and equality before position \\(\\min(p,q)\\). If \\(p=q\\), join the final letters in \\(D\\), or obtain an empty intersection. If \\(p&lt;q\\), a common multiple exists exactly when \\(z_p\\) divides \\(t_p\\) in \\(D\\); then the first word divides the second, which is their join. The other case is symmetric.</p><p>An old \\(x\\) and a positive-length word with head \\(y\\) have a common multiple exactly when \\(x\\sqsubseteq y\\); then \\(x\\) divides that word. Two old elements use their old join. These descriptions give conditional joins and preserve old comparisons, quotients, and nonjoins. The words \\(l_i x\\) have exactly the prescribed identifications.</p><p>For margins, let \\(r\\) be the number of copies, and define\n\\[\n\\rho_D(z)=\\frac1r\\sum_{i&lt;r}\\rho_i(z),\\qquad\n\\rho_i(z)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\rho(x),&amp;z=t_i x,\\\\\n0,&amp;z\\text{ has no representation in copy }i.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nEach coordinate is nondecreasing along a comparison; some coordinate increases strictly in a proper comparison. Choose positive costs satisfying\n\\[\n\\epsilon_z^D\\leq\n\\frac1r\\min\\bigl(\n\\{\\rho(1)/2\\}\\cup\n\\{\\epsilon_x:z=t_i x\\text{ for some }i\\}\n\\bigr).\n\\]\nFor \\(u&lt;v\\) in \\(D\\), classify a divisor \\(d\\) of \\(v\\) missing \\(u\\) by a copy representing \\(d\\). That copy also represents \\(v\\). If it represents \\(u\\), its old margin bounds the cost supremum strictly below its coordinate increase divided by \\(r\\). Otherwise that increase is at least \\(\\rho(1)\\), while the costs are at most \\(\\rho(1)/(2r)\\). Finitely many strict bounds give the required strict bound for \\(\\rho_D(v)-\\rho_D(u)\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\theta,\\phi&gt;0\\) with \\(\\theta+\\phi&lt;1\\), and set recursively\n\\[\n\\rho(wz)=\\rho(w)+(1-\\rho(w))(\\theta+\\phi\\rho_D(z)),\n\\]\n\\[\n0&lt;\\epsilon_{wz}\\leq\n(1-\\rho(w))\\min(\\theta/2,\\phi\\epsilon_z^D).\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]\nFor comparable new words of equal length, only their last letters vary; missed divisors of that length are handled by the scaled \\(D\\)-margin, and shorter divisors divide both words. Comparing \\(w\\) with \\(wz\\), every missed divisor has the new length and prefix \\(w\\), so its cost is at most \\((1-\\rho(w))\\theta/2\\), strictly below the height increase.</p><p>An arbitrary comparison factors through these comparisons and, when the lower word is old, an old comparison of leading coefficients. Transitivity of the margin, established in Lemma 1.3, finishes the verification. Old cost suprema are unchanged because no new element divides an old one.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "Lemma 2.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B\\subseteq M\\) is conservative.</p><p class=\"li\">Both monoids are left cancellative and conical and have conditional joins.</p><p class=\"li\">A probe operation or finite-merger operation has parameters in \\(B\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The operation is valid over \\(M\\), and its extension of \\(B\\) conservatively embeds into the corresponding extension of \\(M\\), using the same generators. A finite overlap prescription with parameters in \\(B\\) is valid over \\(B\\) if and only if it is valid over \\(M\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For validity of an overlap prescription, the only nontrivial transitivity check passes through three copies. In the middle copy, intersect the two overlap starts. Conservativity preserves disjointness, or preserves the old join and its right quotients. Every later common multiple is a multiple of that join. The endpoint identifications agree at the join in a valid prescription over \\(B\\), hence agree at every later common multiple. Conversely, restrict a valid prescription to \\(B\\). Comparisons of its parameters have quotients in \\(B\\), so no identification or transitivity witness between elements of \\(B\\) requires leaving \\(B\\).</p><p>For probes, equality of letters in the smaller direct limit is preserved and reflected because it is equality at a finite common stage. If an old \\(b\\in B\\) divides a smaller letter \\([x,i]\\) in the larger direct limit, then at a common stage\n\\[\nby=xd_i\\cdots d_{j-1}.\n\\]\nBoth endpoints belong to \\(B\\), so conservativity puts \\(y\\) in \\(B\\). Divisibility by a smaller old multiplier and its quotient are therefore unchanged. The same irreducible words embed.</p><p>The gate recursion uses only earlier gates, old joins, and old quotients. It consequently has the same outcome, including absence of a gate. The join calculations of Lemma 1.2 preserve and reflect joins and nonjoins.</p><p>Right quotients between new forms are conserved by division. If both forms are old, use conservativity of the base. If the divisor is old and the dividend new, divide its first direct-limit letter by the common-stage calculation and retain its suffix. If both have the same new head, cancel and recurse. In the remaining cases, a new divisor leaves its first-letter block: factor through its gate using (2.11), then divide that old gate into the dividend. All factors lie in the smaller extension, and cancellation makes their product the unique quotient.</p><p>For mergers, overlap representation and cone intersections in \\(D\\) were calculated solely using old joins and their right quotients. These calculations are unchanged. Normal forms (2.15) therefore preserve comparisons, their quotients, joins, and nonjoins.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "Lemma 2.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">A probe satisfies (2.6) at one stage of a tower of the two extension operations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>It continues to satisfy (2.6) at every later stage and at the union, with the full later downward closure of its original path. Previously constructed joins between probes are preserved.</p>", "proof": "<p>A merger introduces no new divisor of the old \\(a\\). At a probe extension, a new \\(x\\sqsubseteq a\\) has an old gate \\(g(x)\\sqsubseteq a\\). If \\(x\\) also divides the old probe, its gate lies below that probe and hence below an original path element; then \\(x\\) does too.</p><p>If \\(x\\) does not divide the old probe, neither does its gate. The old probe cannot divide \\(x\\), because it does not divide \\(a\\). The gate calculation therefore gives join \\(ae\\). This proves both alternatives at a successor stage.</p><p>At a limit, each tested element has already appeared, and comparisons and joins have been preserved. Conservativity directly preserves joins between existing probes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemma 2.8", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(T\\) satisfies Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">\\((n_i)_{i&lt;\\omega}\\) is nondecreasing in \\(T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a countable-base tower from Proposition 1 whose initial submonoid \\(T_0\\) contains all \\(n_i\\) and all right quotients between them.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B\\subseteq\\omega\\times T\\) satisfies:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. for every \\((l,m)\\in B\\), some \\(N\\) satisfies \\(m\\sqsubseteq n_N\\);</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. for every \\((l,m)\\in B\\) and every \\(N\\), there is \\((l',md)\\in B\\) with\n     \\[\n     l'\\geq l,\\qquad n_l d=n_{l'},\\qquad n_N\\sqsubseteq md.\n     \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Starting from any pair in \\(B\\), finitely many such replacements reach a pair whose second coordinate belongs to \\(T_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(m\\notin T_0\\), consider its first birth step in the reordered tower. It has an old multiple \\(n_N\\), so this is a probe step and its gate satisfies \\(g(m)\\sqsubseteq n_N\\).</p><p>Choose an admissible replacement with \\(n_N\\sqsubseteq md\\). The quotient \\(d\\), being a right quotient between path elements, belongs to \\(T_0\\), so it is old at the birth step of \\(m\\). Conservativity and (2.3) imply that \\(md\\) belongs to the strictly earlier base.</p><p>Repeat if necessary. Each repetition strictly decreases the birth ordinal, so the process terminates after finitely many replacements, with second coordinate in \\(T_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemmas 3.1 and 3.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Work in a ground model \\(V\\models\\mathrm{ZFC}\\). All parameters and cardinal calculations in this statement are in \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\kappa=\\aleph_1^V,\\qquad\n  \\mathfrak c=(2^{\\aleph_0})^V,\\qquad\n  \\mu=(\\mathfrak c^+)^V,\\qquad |I|^V=\\mu^+.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Take the monoid \\(T\\), with distinguished \\(e\\), from Proposition 1 using \\(\\mu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb R=\\operatorname{Add}(\\kappa,I),\\qquad\n  \\mathbb P=\\{r:T\\hookrightarrow I\\},\\qquad\n  \\mathbb Q=\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb P.\n  \\]\n  Conditions in \\(\\mathbb R\\) are partial functions \\(I\\times\\kappa\\to2\\) with countable domain, ordered by reverse inclusion. Put\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{supp}_I(d)=\n  \\{j\\in I:(\\exists\\alpha&lt;\\kappa)\\,(j,\\alpha)\\in\\operatorname{dom}(d)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(r\\in\\mathbb P\\) and \\(n\\in T\\), define\n  \\[\n  r_n(x)=r(nx),\\qquad D_r=r[T],\n  \\qquad r\\leq_{\\mathbb P}s\\Longleftrightarrow(\\exists n\\in T)\\,s=r_n.\n  \\]\n  The center of \\(r\\) is \\(r(1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The notation \\(\\mathbb P\\) always refers to this ground set, including in an \\(\\mathbb R\\)-extension.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The forcing \\(\\mathbb R\\) is countably closed and \\(\\mu\\)-cc, adds no countable sequences of ground elements, preserves \\(\\kappa\\), and preserves every ground cardinal at least \\(\\mu\\). If \\(V\\models\\mathrm{CH}\\), it preserves all cardinals.</p><p>The diagram forcing has the following extension and amalgamation properties:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For \\(p\\in\\mathbb P\\) and ground \\(J\\subseteq I\\) with \\(|J|\\leq\\mu\\), some \\(r\\in\\mathbb P\\) satisfies \\(r_e=p\\) and \\(J\\subseteq D_r\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. A finite family \\(s_i\\in\\mathbb P\\) has a common extension if every pair has disjoint ranges or intersection exactly the range of a common subdiagram\n   \\[\n   s_{i,a_{ij}}=s_{j,a_{ji}}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. A partial placement of finitely many diagrams, injective and consistent on their specified common parts, extends to a ground permutation of \\(I\\). Unspecified coordinates of those diagrams and finitely many Cohen conditions may be placed outside any prescribed ground subset of \\(I\\) of size at most \\(\\mu\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The cardinal calculation in Lemma 3.1 permits use of Proposition 1. Left cancellation makes every \\(r_n\\) injective. Centers determine the translating element whenever \\(s=r_n\\). If \\(r=r_{nm}\\), injectivity and conicality imply \\(n=m=1\\), proving antisymmetry; transitivity follows from \\((r_n)_m=r_{nm}\\).</p><p>Lemma 3.1 proves the forcing assertions, and Lemma 3.2 proves the diagram assertions. No closure or cardinal-preservation assertion about \\(\\mathbb P\\) or the full product is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.\nUse the forcing and cardinal parameters of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The forcing \\(\\mathbb R\\) has all the closure, chain-condition, and preservation properties asserted in Proposition 3.</p>", "proof": "<p>For every infinite cardinal \\(\\lambda&lt;\\mu\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda^{\\aleph_0}\\leq\\mathfrak c^{\\aleph_0}\n=\\mathfrak c&lt;\\mu.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mu\\) is regular uncountable, every countable sequence in \\(\\mu\\) is bounded, and hence\n\\[\n\\mu^{\\aleph_0}=\\mu.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]</p><p>A descending countable sequence of Cohen conditions has its union as a lower bound. Deciding successive entries of a name for a sequence in a ground set and taking such a lower bound shows that no countable sequence in that set is added. This preserves \\(\\omega_1^V\\).</p><p>For the chain condition, a family of \\(\\mu\\) countable sets has a delta-system subfamily of size \\(\\mu\\). Code its union into \\(\\mu\\), and write its members as \\(S_\\alpha\\subseteq\\mu\\), \\(\\alpha&lt;\\mu\\). There is a club of \\(\\delta&lt;\\mu\\) such that \\(S_\\alpha\\subseteq\\delta\\) whenever \\(\\alpha&lt;\\delta\\). On its stationary part of cofinality \\(\\omega_1\\), each \\(S_\\alpha\\cap\\alpha\\) is bounded in \\(\\alpha\\). Pressing down gives a constant bound \\(\\gamma&lt;\\mu\\) on a stationary subset. There are fewer than \\(\\mu\\) countable subsets of \\(\\gamma\\), so regularity makes \\(S_\\alpha\\cap\\alpha\\) constant on a stationary subset. For \\(\\alpha&lt;\\beta\\) there, \\(S_\\alpha\\subseteq\\beta\\), so their intersection is this fixed root.</p><p>Apply this to domains of \\(\\mu\\) Cohen conditions. There are at most \\(2^{\\aleph_0}=\\mathfrak c&lt;\\mu\\) assignments on the countable root. Thin again to \\(\\mu\\) conditions agreeing on the root. They are compatible, proving the \\(\\mu\\)-chain condition.</p><p>The usual antichain bound preserves ground cardinals at least \\(\\mu\\): possible values at one coordinate of an ordinal-valued name are covered by fewer than \\(\\mu\\) ground ordinals, with regularity handling \\(\\mu\\) itself. Together with countable closure, this preserves all cardinals when \\(\\mu=\\aleph_2\\). No preservation of cardinals strictly between \\(\\kappa\\) and \\(\\mu\\) is asserted in a general ground.</p><p>Finally, a sequence of ground elements has values in a ground rank segment, since their ranks have an ordinal bound and forcing preserves ordinals. Thus preservation for sequences in ground sets covers all countable sequences of ground elements.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.\nUse \\(T,I,\\mathbb P,\\mathbb R\\) from Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The three diagram extension, finite amalgamation, and permutation-placement assertions of Proposition 3 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first assertion, Proposition 1 gives \\(\\mu\\) probes for \\(a=1\\), \\(b=e\\), \\(U=\\{1\\}\\), satisfying \\(me=e\\). None belongs to \\(eT\\): if \\(m=ex\\), cancellation gives \\(xe=1\\), contradicting conicality and \\(e\\neq1\\). Thus \\(|T\\setminus eT|=\\mu\\).</p><p>Define \\(r(ex)=p(x)\\), using left cancellation, and fill the complement injectively in \\(I\\setminus D_p\\), including \\(J\\setminus D_p\\). The size of \\(T\\setminus eT\\) and \\(\\mu&lt;|I|\\) permit this.</p><p>For the second assertion, identify copies of \\(T\\) exactly when their labels in \\(I\\) coincide. The hypotheses give a finite overlap prescription; actual equality of labels ensures validity. Let \\(l_i\\in T\\) realize it by Proposition 1. On \\(\\bigcup_i l_iT\\), assign \\(l_i x\\) the value \\(s_i(x)\\). Exact overlap makes this well defined and injective. Extend injectively to all of \\(T\\). The resulting \\(r\\) satisfies \\(r_{l_i}=s_i\\).</p><p>For the last assertion, the prescribed domain and range have size at most \\(\\mu\\), and both complements in \\(I\\) have size \\(\\mu^+\\). A bijection of complements extends the partial bijection to a permutation. Fresh images may first be chosen off the excluded set of size at most \\(\\mu\\), and the same argument then applies. All choices are in \\(V\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Theorem 3.3; symmetric-system definitions from Section 3.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the ground model and product forcing \\(\\mathbb Q=\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb P\\) of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal G=\\operatorname{Sym}(I)^V\\), acting by\n  \\[\n  (\\pi r)(x)=\\pi(r(x)),\\qquad\n  (\\pi d)(\\pi j,\\alpha)=d(j,\\alpha).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For ground \\(J\\subseteq I\\), let \\(\\operatorname{Fix}(J)\\) be its pointwise stabilizer in \\(\\mathcal G\\), and put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F=\n  \\{K\\leq\\mathcal G:\n  (\\exists J\\in V)\\,[J\\subseteq I,\\ |J|^V\\leq\\mu,\\\n  \\operatorname{Fix}(J)\\subseteq K]\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The action on names is recursive:\n  \\[\n  \\pi\\tau=\\{(\\pi\\sigma,\\pi u):(\\sigma,u)\\in\\tau\\}.\n  \\]\n  A name has support \\(J\\) if \\(\\operatorname{Fix}(J)\\) fixes it literally. A name is hereditarily symmetric, abbreviated \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\), if it has a support and all its member names are hereditarily symmetric.</p><p class=\"li\">Ground check names are fixed by every permutation. Canonical ordered-pair and sequence names commute with the action. If needed, adjoin a fixed largest condition.</p><p class=\"li\">In an externally transitive presentation, let \\(F\\) be \\(\\mathbb R\\)-generic over \\(V\\), let \\(H\\) be generic on the ground \\(\\mathbb P\\) over \\(V[F]\\), and set\n  \\[\n  W=\\{\\tau^{F\\times H}:\\tau\\in\\mathrm{HS}^V\\}.\n  \\]\n  Write \\(\\tau[F]\\) for the ordinary \\(\\mathbb P\\)-name obtained by evaluating the Cohen part.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The symmetric forcing relation is definable formula by formula in \\(V\\), and forces every axiom instance of full pure-set \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\). For externally transitive \\(V\\), \\(W\\) is transitive,\n\\[\nV\\subseteq W\\subseteq V[F][H],\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Ord}^{W}=\\operatorname{Ord}^{V[F][H]},\n\\]\nand the truth lemma holds.</p><p>For an arbitrary countable choice ground, the corresponding assertion holds for the symmetric quotient of internal names.</p><p>The product symmetry identities are\n\\[\n(d,s)\\Vdash_{\\mathbb Q}\\varphi(\\vec\\tau)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n(\\pi d,\\pi s)\\Vdash_{\\mathbb Q}\\varphi(\\pi\\vec\\tau),\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(\\pi\\tau)^{\\pi F\\times\\pi H}=\\tau^{F\\times H}.\n\\]\nThese identities do not define an automorphism of one fixed evaluated extension.</p>", "proof": "<p>The displayed actions are automorphisms of \\(\\mathbb Q\\). The subgroup collection \\(\\mathcal F\\) is a normal filter: finite intersections are handled by unions of supports, and\n\\[\n\\pi\\operatorname{Fix}(J)\\pi^{-1}=\\operatorname{Fix}(\\pi J).\n\\]</p><p>Apply the set-forcing symmetric-extension theorem, as cited in the paper at [11, Section 2.1], to \\(\\mathbb Q,\\mathcal G,\\mathcal F\\). Its internal formulation uses all hereditarily symmetric names and yields full Separation, Replacement, and Foundation. No countable-completeness hypothesis on the filter is required.</p><p>External transitivity permits recursive name evaluation and gives the literal inclusions. Over an arbitrary countable ground, the internal-name quotient gives truth without asserting external well-foundedness.</p><p>The usual symmetry lemma gives (3.2), and recursive evaluation gives the evaluation identity. Product/iteration equivalence also gives its factorized form for Cohen conditions forcing statements of the form\n\\[\ns\\Vdash_{\\mathbb P}\\varphi(\\vec\\tau[F]).\n\\]\nHere \\(\\pi s\\) is explicitly reindexed ground data, not the image of \\(\\check s\\), which is fixed.</p><p>For later use, let \\(\\dot A_j\\) be the canonical name for the Cohen subset of \\(\\kappa\\) at coordinate \\(j\\), and put\n\\[\nA_j=\\dot A_j^{F\\times H},\\qquad A=\\{A_j:j\\in I\\}.\n\\]\nThe name \\(\\dot A_j\\) has support \\(\\{j\\}\\), \\(\\pi\\dot A_j=\\dot A_{\\pi j}\\), and the name for \\(A\\) is invariant. Distinct coordinates give distinct labels: below any Cohen condition, assign different bits at an ordinal unused at the two coordinates. Forced distinctness persists after the second forcing.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemmas 3.4 and 3.5; normalization from (3.5)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Setting and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Setting and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the symmetric system of Proposition 4, with \\(V\\models\\mathrm{ZFC}\\), \\(\\mathbb Q=\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb P\\), group \\(\\mathcal G\\), and labels \\(A_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an \\(\\mathbb R\\)-generic filter \\(F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(d\\upharpoonright D_s\\) for \\(d\\upharpoonright(D_s\\times\\kappa)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Supporting diagrams decide ordinary \\(\\mathbb P\\)-forcing assertions with supported product-name parameters, Cohen witnesses may be restricted to supporting coordinates, and equality of values on independent diagrams descends to their common supporting diagram, as precisely stated in Lemmas 5.1 and 5.2.</p><p>For every \\(r\\in\\mathbb P\\), the ground set\n\\[\n\\mathcal X(r)=\n\\left\\{\n\\tau\\subseteq\\{\\dot A_j:j\\in I\\}\\times\\mathbb Q:\n(\\forall\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Fix}(D_r))\\,\\pi\\tau=\\tau\n\\right\\}\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nconsists of hereditarily symmetric names. Every product \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name supported by \\(D_r\\) whose actual value is a subset of \\(A\\) has the same value as a member of \\(\\mathcal X(r)\\), without a restriction on its original rank.</p>", "proof": "<p>The decision and descent assertions are Lemmas 5.1 and 5.2.</p><p>For normalization, if a product \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name \\(\\tau\\) is supported by \\(J\\), define\n\\[\n\\tau\\upharpoonright A=\n\\{(\\dot A_j,u):j\\in I,\\ u\\in\\mathbb Q,\\\nu\\Vdash_{\\mathbb Q}\\dot A_j\\in\\tau\\}.\n\\]\nThe ordinary truth lemma gives value \\(\\tau^{F\\times H}\\cap A\\). Symmetry preserves the support \\(J\\). Its only member names are label names, so it is hereditarily symmetric. For \\(J=D_r\\), it belongs to \\(\\mathcal X(r)\\). This is a set-sized normalization of an original name of any rank.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.\nInherit Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(p\\in\\mathbb P\\) and the product-name parameters \\(\\tau_1,\\ldots,\\tau_k\\) are supported by \\(D_p\\), then in \\(V[F]\\), \\(p\\) decides every ordinary \\(\\mathbb P\\)-forcing formula\n   \\[\n   \\varphi(\\tau_1[F],\\ldots,\\tau_k[F]).\n   \\]\n   The names may have arbitrary rank.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If a Cohen condition \\(d\\) forces\n   \\[\n   s\\Vdash_{\\mathbb P}\\varphi(\\vec\\tau[F]),\n   \\]\n   where all product-name parameters are supported by \\(D_s\\), then \\(d\\upharpoonright D_s\\) forces the same assertion.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first assertion, if decision fails, there are \\(s_0,s_1\\leq p\\) forcing contrary answers in \\(V[F]\\). Choose \\(d_0,d_1\\in F\\) witnessing these answers in the factorized forcing relation. Their restrictions to \\(D_p\\times\\kappa\\) are compatible.</p><p>By Proposition 3, choose \\(\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Fix}(D_p)\\) moving the coordinates of \\(s_1,d_1\\) outside \\(D_p\\) away from those of \\(s_0,d_0\\). The diagrams \\(s_0,\\pi s_1\\) meet exactly in their common subdiagram \\(p\\), and have a common extension. The Cohen conditions \\(d_0,\\pi d_1\\) agree compatibly on the root and have disjoint supports off it. All parameter names are fixed. Product symmetry gives contrary assertions forced by compatible product conditions, a contradiction. It does not assert \\(\\pi d_1\\in F\\).</p><p>For the second assertion, let \\(\\delta=d\\upharpoonright D_s\\). If it does not force the indicated assertion, some \\(c\\leq\\delta\\) forces its negation. Choose \\(\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Fix}(D_s)\\) moving \\(\\operatorname{supp}_I(d)\\setminus D_s\\) off \\(\\operatorname{supp}_I(c)\\setminus D_s\\). Then \\(\\pi d\\) is compatible with \\(c\\), and forces the original assertion because \\(s\\) and the parameter names are fixed. This is a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(s_0,s_1\\leq p\\) and \\(D_{s_0}\\cap D_{s_1}=D_p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The product \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) names \\(\\sigma_i\\) are supported by \\(D_{s_i}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Some \\(v\\leq s_0,s_1\\) forces\n  \\[\n  \\sigma_0[F]=\\sigma_1[F]\n  \\]\n  in ordinary \\(\\mathbb P\\)-forcing over \\(V[F]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a product \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name \\(\\sigma\\), supported by \\(D_p\\), such that\n\\[\ns_i\\Vdash_{\\mathbb P}\\sigma[F]=\\sigma_i[F]\n\\qquad(i=0,1).\n\\]\nThe names may have arbitrary rank. If each \\(s_i\\) also forces \\(\\sigma_i[F]\\subseteq A\\), the descended name can be normalized to a subset name having only label names as possible members.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(d\\in F\\) witnessing the equality at \\(v\\), and restrict it to \\(D_v\\) by Lemma 5.1. Put \\(\\delta=d\\upharpoonright D_{s_0}\\).</p><p>Allow both generic factors to vary. For \\(\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Fix}(D_p)\\), call \\(\\pi\\sigma_0\\) active when its trigger \\((\\pi\\delta,\\pi s_0)\\) belongs to the product generic. We prove that active copies have equal values.</p><p>First suppose two copies have diagrams \\(\\bar s,\\bar s'\\) whose ranges intersect exactly in \\(D_p\\). If they can be unequal while active, a product condition \\((c,t)\\) extends both triggers and forces inequality.</p><p>Place a copy \\(u\\) of \\(s_1\\) over \\(p\\), with its remaining range fresh relative to \\(D_t\\cup\\operatorname{supp}_I(c)\\). Extend the placements of \\((s_0,s_1)\\) onto \\((\\bar s,u)\\) and \\((\\bar s',u)\\) to placements of \\(v\\), obtaining \\(v',v''\\). Use the same placement on all of \\(D_{s_1}\\), and make remaining coordinates fresh, so that\n\\[\nD_{v'}\\cap D_t=D_{\\bar s},\\qquad\nD_{v''}\\cap D_t=D_{\\bar s'},\\qquad\nD_{v'}\\cap D_{v''}=D_u.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nAll intersections are ranges of the indicated common subdiagrams. Proposition 3 supplies the ground permutations realizing the placements.</p><p>The two placed copies of \\(d\\) agree on \\(D_u\\). They agree with \\(c\\) on \\(D_{\\bar s}\\) and \\(D_{\\bar s'}\\), respectively, because \\(c\\) extends the placed triggers. Freshness gives compatibility elsewhere. The three diagrams \\(v',v'',t\\) have a common extension by finite amalgamation.</p><p>The transported \\(\\sigma_1\\) names are literally equal because the placements agree on \\(D_{s_1}\\). The equalities transported from \\((d,v)\\) contradict the proposed inequality.</p><p>For arbitrary overlap of two active copies, begin with a proposed inequality-forcing common extension \\((c,t)\\). Place a third copy of \\(s_0\\) over \\(p\\), independent of \\(t\\) off the root, with Cohen trigger compatible with \\(c\\). It has a common product extension with \\((c,t)\\). The independent case applies between the third copy and each original copy, again contradicting inequality.</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\sigma=\n\\left\\{\n(\\pi\\eta,(c,t)):\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Fix}(D_p),\\\n(\\eta,(a,u))\\in\\sigma_0,\\\\\n(c,t)\\leq(\\pi\\delta,\\pi s_0),\\\n(c,t)\\leq(\\pi a,\\pi u)\n\\end{array}\n\\right\\}.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nThis is a set name, fixed by \\(\\operatorname{Fix}(D_p)\\). Its member names are translates of \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) member names of \\(\\sigma_0\\).</p><p>Its value is the union of the values of active copies. Both inclusions follow from directedness, since all common strengthenings occur in (3.4). When the original trigger is active, coherence makes this union equal to the value of \\(\\sigma_0\\).</p><p>Return to the fixed \\(F\\). Since \\(\\delta\\in F\\), the original trigger is active whenever \\(s_0\\) belongs to the diagram generic. Thus\n\\[\ns_0\\Vdash_{\\mathbb P}\\sigma[F]=\\sigma_0[F].\n\\]\nEquality with \\(\\sigma_1[F]\\) holds below \\(v\\). Its parameters are supported by \\(D_{s_1}\\), so Lemma 5.1 makes it hold already below \\(s_1\\).</p><p>No rank bound was imposed. In the subset case, replace \\(\\sigma\\) by its normalized intersection with \\(A\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 3.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the symmetric model \\(W\\) and labels \\(A=\\{A_j:j\\in I\\}\\) of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In \\(W\\), every nonempty set \\(X\\) is the image of a surjection\n\\[\nA\\times\\eta\\twoheadrightarrow X\n\\]\nfor some ordinal \\(\\eta\\). No bound on the rank of \\(X\\) is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\sigma\\) be a ground \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name, supported by \\(D_p\\). Form the graph name\n\\[\n\\dot h_{\\sigma,p}=\n\\left\\{\n\\bigl(\\operatorname{op}(\\dot A_{\\pi(p(1))},\\pi\\sigma),\n(1_{\\mathbb R},\\pi p)\\bigr):\n\\pi\\in\\mathcal G\n\\right\\},\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\operatorname{op}\\) is the canonical ordered-pair name. This name is invariant and has \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) member names.</p><p>Its value is a partial function on \\(A\\). If two contributing pairs have equal first coordinates, label distinctness gives \\(\\pi(p(1))=\\rho(p(1))\\). A common \\(r\\in H\\) extends \\(\\pi p,\\rho p\\); write \\(\\pi p=r_n\\), \\(\\rho p=r_m\\). Equality of centers and injectivity of \\(r\\) give \\(n=m\\), hence \\(\\pi p=\\rho p\\) pointwise. Thus \\(\\rho^{-1}\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Fix}(D_p)\\) fixes \\(\\sigma\\), and \\(\\pi\\sigma=\\rho\\sigma\\) literally. Equal inputs have equal values.</p><p>Choose an \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name \\(\\tau\\) for \\(X\\), and let \\(M\\) be its ground set of member names. In \\(V\\), enumerate by an ordinal \\(\\eta\\) the set\n\\[\nB=\\{(\\sigma,p)\\in M\\times\\mathbb P:\n\\operatorname{Fix}(D_p)\\text{ fixes }\\sigma\\}.\n\\]\nEvery member name has a support of size at most \\(\\mu\\), and Proposition 3 supplies supporting diagrams. All such diagrams are included, since one chosen diagram need not belong to the generic.</p><p>The corresponding sequence of invariant graph names has an invariant hereditarily symmetric sequence name. Its interpretation \\(\\langle h_i:i&lt;\\eta\\rangle\\) belongs to \\(W\\).</p><p>These partial functions cover \\(X\\). For \\(x\\in X\\), an active member name \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\tau\\) has value \\(x\\). Diagrams whose ranges contain a fixed support of \\(\\sigma\\) form a ground dense set. Some such \\(p\\) lies in \\(H\\), and the identity entry of \\(\\dot h_{\\sigma,p}\\) sends \\(A_{p(1)}\\) to \\(x\\).</p><p>Inside \\(W\\), fix one \\(x_0\\in X\\), and define\n\\[\nh(a,i)=\n\\begin{cases}\nh_i(a),&amp;a\\in\\operatorname{dom}(h_i)\\text{ and }h_i(a)\\in X,\\\\\nx_0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nSeparation and Replacement give this function on \\(A\\times\\eta\\), and coverage makes it surjective. Only one default element was chosen. The enumeration depends on the set of member names of \\(\\tau\\), not on a fixed rank bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 3.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the symmetric model \\(W\\) of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The model \\(W\\) satisfies \\(\\mathrm{AC}_{\\mathrm{WO}}\\): every ordinal-indexed family of nonempty sets in \\(W\\) has a choice function in \\(W\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First consider a family \\(\\langle X_\\xi:\\xi&lt;\\eta\\rangle\\) of nonempty subsets of \\(A\\). Start below a product condition \\((d_0,p)\\) forcing this assertion for an \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) family name. Extend the diagram so that \\(D_p\\) contains its support and \\(\\operatorname{supp}_I(d_0)\\).</p><p>Functionhood and nonempty-subset values are absolute between \\(W\\) and the full extension, so the ordinary-forcing assertion follows from a symmetric-forcing hypothesis. Names for individual values \\(X_\\xi\\), obtained from the family and \\(\\check\\xi\\) by membership forcing, retain support \\(D_p\\).</p><p>Fix \\(q\\) with \\(q_e=p\\). This same \\(q\\) will work for every \\(\\xi\\).</p><p>Fix \\(F\\ni d_0\\) and \\(\\xi&lt;\\eta\\). In \\(V[F]\\), some \\(t\\leq p\\) and \\(j\\in I\\) satisfy\n\\[\nt\\Vdash_{\\mathbb P}A_j\\in X_\\xi.\n\\]\nDecide a representative index in the ground set \\(I\\), and extend \\(t\\) so \\(j\\in D_t\\). Write \\(p=t_b\\). Choose \\(d\\in F\\) witnessing this assertion and restrict \\(d\\) to \\(D_t\\) by Proposition 5.</p><p>If \\(b=1\\), then \\(t=p\\), and \\(q\\) already forces a witness indexed in \\(D_q\\). If \\(b\\neq1\\), Proposition 1 gives \\(\\mu\\) probes \\(m\\) for \\(a=1,U=\\{1\\}\\), with\n\\[\nmb=e,\\qquad mT\\cap m'T=eT\\quad(m\\neq m').\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n(q_m)_b=p,\\qquad\nD_{q_m}\\cap D_{q_{m'}}=D_p.\n\\]\nEach \\(q_m\\) is a copy of \\(t\\) over \\(p\\). Choose ground\n\\(\\pi_m\\in\\operatorname{Fix}(D_p)\\) taking \\(t\\) to \\(q_m\\). The conditions \\(\\pi_m d\\) have common restriction\n\\[\nd^*=d\\upharpoonright D_p\n\\]\nand pairwise disjoint nonroot coordinate supports.</p><p>The ground set\n\\[\n\\{c:c\\perp d^*\\}\\cup\n\\{c:(\\exists m)\\ c\\leq\\pi_m d\\}\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nis dense in \\(\\mathbb R\\). If \\(c\\) is compatible with \\(d^*\\), extend by \\(d^*\\). Its countable support meets only countably many disjoint nonroot ranges, so choose a remaining probe and extend by its placed condition.</p><p>Since \\(d^*\\in F\\), genericity gives \\(\\pi_m d\\in F\\) for some \\(m\\). Product symmetry and \\(\\pi_m X_\\xi=X_\\xi\\) give\n\\[\nq_m\\Vdash_{\\mathbb P}A_{\\pi_m j}\\in X_\\xi\n\\]\nin \\(V[F]\\). Since \\(q\\leq q_m\\) and \\(\\pi_m j\\in D_q\\), \\(q\\) forces a witness in\n\\[\nA[D_q]=\\{A_j:j\\in D_q\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Although witnesses \\(t,d,j\\) were chosen after \\(F\\), each is an individual ground object; the dense set (3.7) belongs to \\(V\\). No sequence of witnesses for all indices is imported into \\(W\\).</p><p>For each \\(\\xi\\), \\(q\\) forces \\(X_\\xi\\cap A[D_q]\\neq\\varnothing\\). It forces the universal statement: a condition forcing a counterexample could be strengthened to decide its ordinal index to a ground \\(\\xi\\), a contradiction. Thus \\((d_0,q)\\) forces that \\(A[D_q]\\) meets every \\(X_\\xi\\).</p><p>A ground enumeration of \\(D_q\\) yields an \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) sequence of its labels supported by \\(D_q\\). Label distinctness turns this into a wellordering of \\(A[D_q]\\) in \\(W\\). Choosing the first element of each intersection gives a choice function by Replacement. The argument works below an extension of every starting condition.</p><p>Finally let \\(\\langle Y_\\xi:\\xi&lt;\\eta\\rangle\\) be any family of nonempty sets in \\(W\\). If \\(\\eta=0\\), use the empty function. Otherwise Proposition 6, applied once to its union, gives\n\\[\nh:A\\times\\theta\\twoheadrightarrow\\bigcup_{\\xi&lt;\\eta}Y_\\xi.\n\\]\nFor each \\(\\xi\\), take the least \\(\\alpha_\\xi&lt;\\theta\\) whose slice meets \\(Y_\\xi\\), and define\n\\[\nB_\\xi=\\{a\\in A:h(a,\\alpha_\\xi)\\in Y_\\xi\\}.\n\\]\nThe subset case chooses \\(a_\\xi\\in B_\\xi\\). Then \\(h(a_\\xi,\\alpha_\\xi)\\) is the desired choice.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Propositions 3.8 and 3.9", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the symmetric model \\(W\\), ground \\(V\\), and label set \\(A\\) of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The set \\(A\\) is not wellorderable in \\(W\\), so \\(W\\models\\neg\\mathrm{AC}\\).</p><p>For every ground set \\(X\\), every function \\(f:\\omega\\to X\\) belonging to \\(W\\) belongs to \\(V\\). Consequently \\(W\\) adds no countable sequences of ground elements.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.1 gives nonwellorderability and failure of Choice. Lemma 8.2 gives preservation for sequences in each ground set. Any countable sequence of ground elements has its range in a ground rank segment, by an ordinal bound on their ranks, so the latter assertion covers all countable sequences of ground elements.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Proposition 3.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.\nInherit Proposition 8.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is no function in \\(W\\) from an ordinal onto \\(A\\). In particular, \\(A\\) is not wellorderable in \\(W\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose an \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name is forced to be a function from an ordinal \\(\\eta\\) onto \\(A\\). Choose \\((d,p)\\) with \\(D_p\\) containing the name’s support and \\(\\operatorname{supp}_I(d)\\).</p><p>Choose \\(j\\notin D_p\\), possible since \\(|D_p|^V=\\mu&lt;|I|^V\\). Strengthen to \\((c,t)\\) deciding a ground \\(\\beta&lt;\\eta\\) whose value is \\(A_j\\), and extend the diagram so \\(j\\in D_t\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Fix}(D_p)\\) moving the coordinates of \\(t,c\\) outside \\(D_p\\) to fresh coordinates. The diagrams \\(t,\\pi t\\) are independent over \\(p\\) and have a common extension; \\(c,\\pi c\\) are compatible. The function name and \\(\\check\\beta\\) are fixed.</p><p>Their common product extension forces the same entry to equal both \\(A_j\\) and \\(A_{\\pi j}\\). These labels are distinct, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Proposition 3.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.\nInherit Proposition 8, and let \\(X\\in V\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(f\\in W\\) is a function \\(\\omega\\to X\\), then \\(f\\in V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a product \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name \\(\\tau\\) for \\(f\\). In \\(V[F]\\), the ordinary \\(\\mathbb P\\)-truth lemma gives a condition in \\(H\\) forcing that \\(\\tau[F]\\) is a function \\(\\check\\omega\\to\\check X\\). Extend within \\(H\\) to \\(p\\) whose range contains a support of \\(\\tau\\).</p><p>For each \\(n&lt;\\omega\\), an extension of \\(p\\) forces \\(\\tau[F](n)=\\check x\\) for some \\(x\\in X\\). The parameters are supported by \\(D_p\\), so Proposition 5 makes \\(p\\) itself force the equality. There is exactly one such \\(x\\).</p><p>Definability of forcing and Replacement in \\(V[F]\\) form the sequence of these unique values, \\(g:\\omega\\to X\\). Since \\(p\\in H\\), \\(g=f\\) in \\(V[F][H]\\). Proposition 3 says that \\(\\mathbb R\\) adds no countable sequence in \\(X\\), so \\(g\\in V\\), and hence \\(f\\in V\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 4.3; supporting labels: Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the ground forcing and symmetric system of Propositions 3–4.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name \\(\\dot E\\) and a product condition \\((d_0,p)\\) symmetrically forcing that \\(\\dot E\\) is an equivalence relation on \\(A\\) with nonwellorderable quotient.</p><p class=\"li\">Arrange that \\(D_p\\) supports \\(\\dot E\\) and contains \\(\\operatorname{supp}_I(d_0)\\). Choose \\(q\\) with \\(q_e=p\\), and put \\(G_q=\\operatorname{Fix}(D_q)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an \\(\\mathbb R\\)-generic \\(F\\ni d_0\\). Forcing in this statement, unless otherwise indicated, is ordinary \\(\\mathbb P\\)-forcing over \\(V[F]\\) on sections of product names.</p><p class=\"li\">For ground \\(r\\leq q\\), let \\(\\mathcal X(r)\\) be (3.5), and define\n  \\[\n  D_F(r)=\n  \\{\\tau\\in\\mathcal X(r):r\\Vdash\\tau\\text{ is an }\\dot E\\text{-class}\\}/\\sim_r,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\tau\\sim_r\\sigma\\Longleftrightarrow r\\Vdash\\tau=\\sigma.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(r\\leq s\\leq q\\), retaining the same name gives an injection\n  \\[\n  \\iota_{s,r}:D_F(s)\\longrightarrow D_F(r).\n  \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(q=r_a\\), define\n  \\[\n  U_h=\\{n\\sqsubseteq a:h\\in\\operatorname{ran}(\\iota_{r_n,r})\\},\n  \\qquad h\\in D_F(r),\n  \\tag{4.2}\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  U_j=\\{n\\sqsubseteq a:j\\in D_{r_n}\\},\n  \\qquad j\\in D_r.\n  \\tag{4.3}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonempty ideal \\(U\\subseteq\\downarrow a\\), put\n  \\[\n  B_{r,U}(F)=\\{h\\in D_F(r):U_h=U\\},\n  \\qquad\n  L_{r,U}=\\{j\\in D_r:U_j=U\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The sets \\(U_h,U_j\\) are ideals, and every ideal of \\(\\downarrow a\\) in \\(V[F]\\) belongs to \\(V\\). For every \\(r\\leq q\\) and every such ideal \\(U\\),\n\\[\n|B_{r,U}(F)|=|L_{r,U}|=\\mu\n\\]\nin \\(V[F]\\); the label fiber \\(L_{r,U}\\) already has cardinality \\(\\mu\\) in \\(V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 5 gives decision for class and equality tests, so the symbolic sets and injections (4.1) are well defined; a stronger diagram cannot newly identify two names already supported at the weaker one. The injections compose literally on representatives. Each \\(j\\in D_r\\) has a normalized \\(D_r\\)-supported name for its \\(\\dot E\\)-class. Lemma 9.2 proves the ideal assertions.</p><p>A class unavailable at the root. Enumerate \\(\\mathcal X(p)\\) in \\(V\\) as \\((\\tau_\\xi)_{\\xi&lt;\\lambda}\\). The canonical sequence of their evaluations has an \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name supported by \\(D_p\\): each entry is the canonical pair \\((\\check\\xi,\\tau_\\xi)\\), under every condition.</p><p>Its range is wellorderable inside \\(W\\), by assigning each value its least occurrence. If it contained every \\(\\dot E\\)-class, this assignment would wellorder the quotient. Thus, ordinarily below \\((d_0,p)\\), some class lies outside the evaluation list.</p><p>Fix \\(F\\ni d_0\\). Choose \\(t\\leq p\\) and \\(j\\in D_t\\) such that \\(t\\) forces the class of \\(A_j\\) outside that list. Choose a witnessing \\(d\\in F\\) supported in \\(D_t\\). Write \\(p=t_b\\). Necessarily \\(b\\neq1\\), since labels in \\(D_p\\) have class names in \\(\\mathcal X(p)\\).</p><p>Independent active copies. Since \\(p=r_{ae}\\), Proposition 1 gives \\(\\mu\\) probes \\(m\\) with\n\\[\nmb=ae,\\qquad\n\\downarrow m\\cap\\downarrow a=U,\n\\]\nand pairwise cone intersection \\(aeT\\). The diagrams \\(r_m\\) are copies of \\(t\\) over \\(p\\), with pairwise range intersection exactly \\(D_p\\). Choose ground permutations taking \\(t\\) to them and fixing \\(D_p\\), and transport \\(d\\) and its class name.</p><p>There are \\(\\mu\\) copies whose transported Cohen conditions belong to \\(F\\). Partition the ground family into \\(\\mu\\) ground subfamilies of size \\(\\mu\\). In each subfamily, conditions extending one transported copy of \\(d\\) are dense below \\(d\\upharpoonright D_p\\): a countable support misses one of the pairwise disjoint nonroot ranges, and root restrictions agree. Genericity meets the dense set for each subfamily, giving \\(\\mu\\) distinct hits.</p><p>Exact quotient fibers. Each hit gives a \\(D_{r_m}\\)-supported class name forced at \\(r_m\\) outside the evaluations of \\(\\mathcal X(p)\\). Although \\(r_m\\) need not extend \\(q\\), its name represents an element of \\(D_F(r)\\), since \\(r\\leq r_m\\).</p><p>Two such classes cannot be equal at \\(r\\): support descent over their common root \\(p\\) would supply a name in \\(\\mathcal X(p)\\), contradicting the outside assertion.</p><p>If \\(n\\in U\\), then \\(n\\sqsubseteq m\\), so \\(r_n\\leq r_m\\), and the class is available at \\(r_n\\). If \\(n\\sqsubseteq a\\) but \\(n\\notin U\\), then\n\\[\nnT\\cap mT=aeT,\\qquad\nD_{r_n}\\cap D_{r_m}=D_p.\n\\]\nAvailability at \\(r_n\\) would descend to \\(\\mathcal X(p)\\), again impossible. Each hit therefore has exact availability ideal \\(U\\). This gives \\(\\mu\\) elements in \\(B_{r,U}(F)\\), and Lemma 9.1 supplies the upper bound.</p><p>Exact label fibers. The centers \\(r(m)\\) have availability ideal\n\\[\n\\downarrow m\\cap\\downarrow a=U.\n\\]\nDistinct probes have distinct centers. This gives a ground \\(\\mu\\)-subset of \\(L_{r,U}\\), while \\(|D_r|=\\mu\\) gives the upper bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.\nInherit Proposition 9.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every ground \\(r\\leq q\\),\n\\[\n|D_F(r)|\\leq\\mu\n\\]\nin \\(V[F]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For an element of \\(D_F(r)\\), choose a representative \\(\\tau\\), a diagram \\(t\\leq r\\), and \\(j\\in D_t\\) such that\n\\[\nt\\Vdash\\tau=[\\dot A_j]_{\\dot E}.\n\\]\nThe forcing theorem decides a representative label, and extending the diagram includes its index. Write \\(r=t_b\\). By Cohen restriction in Proposition 5, choose \\(d\\in F\\), supported in \\(D_t\\), witnessing this assertion.</p><p>Record\n\\[\n(b,x,t^{-1}d),\\qquad t(x)=j,\n\\]\nwhere \\(t^{-1}d\\) is the partial binary function on \\(T\\times\\kappa\\) obtained by replacing \\(t(y)\\) by \\(y\\). Its domain has size \\(&lt;\\kappa\\). There are at most \\(\\mu\\) ground records because\n\\[\n|T|=\\mu,\\qquad\\kappa\\leq\\mu,\\qquad\\mu^{&lt;\\kappa}=\\mu.\n\\]</p><p>Suppose representatives \\(\\tau_1,\\tau_2\\), with witnesses \\(t_i,d_i,j_i\\), have the same record. The bijection \\(t_1(y)\\mapsto t_2(y)\\) is the identity on \\(D_r\\), since \\(r=(t_i)_b\\), and maps points off \\(D_r\\) to points off \\(D_r\\). Extend it to a ground permutation \\(\\pi\\) fixing \\(D_r\\). Then\n\\[\n\\pi t_1=t_2,\\qquad\n\\pi d_1=d_2,\\qquad\n\\pi j_1=j_2,\\qquad\n\\pi\\tau_1=\\tau_1.\n\\]\nProduct symmetry makes \\(d_2\\) witness at \\(t_2\\) that both representatives equal \\([\\dot A_{j_2}]_{\\dot E}\\). Since \\(d_2\\in F\\), \\(t_2\\) forces their equality. Decision at \\(r\\) gives equality already at \\(r\\).</p><p>Thus distinct symbolic elements have distinct chosen records. These choices use Choice in \\(V[F]\\), not in \\(W\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.\nInherit Proposition 9.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(U_h,U_j\\) in (4.2)–(4.3) is an ideal of \\(\\downarrow a\\). Every ideal of \\(\\downarrow a\\) belonging to \\(V[F]\\) belongs to \\(V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Both availability sets contain \\(1\\) and are downward closed by inclusion of subdiagrams.</p><p>Suppose \\(n,n'\\in U_h\\), and let \\(k=n\\vee n'\\). The ranges of \\(r_n,r_{n'}\\) intersect exactly in \\(D_{r_k}\\). Choose supported names representing \\(h\\) on those diagrams; they are forced equal by \\(r\\). Support descent in Proposition 5, with root \\(r_k\\), supplies a \\(D_{r_k}\\)-supported name equal to them. Normalize it without changing support. Decision at \\(r_k\\) shows it is an \\(\\dot E\\)-class there, since a forced negation would persist to \\(r\\). Hence \\(k\\in U_h\\).</p><p>For labels, use the literal equality of cone intersections instead of name descent.</p><p>For any ideal in \\(V[F]\\), apply Lemma 1.1 there. The ground monoid, order, heights, and costs retain their meanings, so the ideal has a countable cofinal sequence of ground elements of \\(T\\). Proposition 3 puts this sequence in \\(V\\). Its downward closure in the unchanged ground order is the original ideal, which therefore belongs to \\(V\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemmas 5.1 and 5.2 and Section 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Keep the fixed quotient setting of Proposition 9: \\(q_e=p\\), \\(F\\ni d_0\\), \\(G_q=\\operatorname{Fix}(D_q)\\), and fibers \\(B_{r,U}(F)\\), \\(L_{r,U}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A vertex is a pair \\(v=(r,U)\\), where \\(r\\leq q\\), \\(q=r_a\\), and \\(U\\) is a nonempty ideal of \\(\\downarrow a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(n\\in U\\), define a contraction\n  \\[\n  (r,U)\\longrightarrow(r_n,U_n),\\qquad\n  U_n=\\{x\\sqsubseteq c:nx\\in U\\},\\quad nc=a.\n  \\tag{5.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A component is a connected component after forgetting contraction directions.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(B_v(F)=B_{r,U}(F)\\), \\(L_v=L_{r,U}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Contractions canonically identify entire exact fibers. Every component \\(C\\) has a countable cofinal chain of contractions, all its label fibers are one ground set \\(L_C\\), and gluing its symbolic fibers by the canonical identifications gives \\(B_C(F)\\), with\n\\[\n|L_C|=|B_C(F)|=\\mu.\n\\]</p><p>The group \\(G_q\\) acts on components. For a cofinal chain based at \\((r,U)\\), with diagrams \\(r_{n_i}\\), let\n\\[\nG_C=\\{\\pi\\in G_q:\\pi C=C\\},\\qquad\nK_C=\\bigcup_{i&lt;\\omega}\\operatorname{Fix}(D_{r_{n_i}}).\n\\]\nThen \\(K_C\\trianglelefteq G_C\\), and in the ground model\n\\[\n|G_C/K_C|\\leq\\aleph_0.\n\\]</p><p>On symbolic fibers, transport between generics is\n\\[\nT_\\pi^F:D_F(r)\\longrightarrow D_{\\pi F}(\\pi r),\n\\qquad\n[\\tau]_r\\longmapsto[\\pi\\tau]_{\\pi r},\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nand it commutes with contraction identifications and their gluing.</p>", "proof": "<p>Vertices form a ground set by Proposition 9. Contractions compose and there is at most one between fixed endpoints, because the center of \\(r_n\\) is \\(r(n)\\) and \\(r\\) is injective.</p><p>Lemmas 10.1–10.2 give canonical fiber gluing and countable cofinal chains. The symmetry lemma gives the transport maps (5.2), and retaining representative names shows that transport commutes with contractions and gluing. Lemma 10.3 proves the stabilizer assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.\nInherit Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every contraction \\(v\\to w\\),\n\\[\nL_v=L_w,\n\\]\nand inclusion of symbolic elements gives a bijection\n\\[\nB_w(F)\\longrightarrow B_v(F).\n\\]\nThese identifications commute with composition.</p>", "proof": "<p>Left multiplication preserves conditional joins:\n\\[\nn(x\\vee y)=nx\\vee ny\n\\]\nwhenever a common right multiple exists, by cancellation and the cone characterization of joins. Thus \\(U_n\\) is an ideal.</p><p>Let \\(v=(r,U)\\), \\(w=(r_n,U_n)\\). For a symbolic element available at \\(r_n\\), let \\(J\\) be its full availability ideal at \\(r\\). It contains \\(n\\), and for \\(z\\sqsubseteq a\\),\n\\[\nz\\in J\\Longleftrightarrow z\\vee n\\in J.\n\\]\nWrite \\(z\\vee n=nx\\). Availability at \\(r_{z\\vee n}\\) is exactly availability at \\((r_n)_x\\). Hence the restricted ideal at \\(r_n\\) determines the full ideal at \\(r\\). If the restricted ideal is \\(U_n\\), this test is equivalent to \\(z\\vee n\\in U\\), hence to \\(z\\in U\\).</p><p>Conversely, every element of \\(B_v(F)\\) is available at \\(r_n\\), since \\(n\\in U\\), and its restricted ideal is \\(U_n\\). This proves surjectivity; injectivity is (4.1).</p><p>The same reasoning with literal range membership proves \\(L_v=L_w\\). Composition commutes because all symbolic maps retain the same names.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "component and cofinal-chain arguments in Section 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.\nInherit Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Any finite set of vertices in a component has a common successor. The identifications of Lemma 10.1 are consistent along finite paths and yield the sets \\(L_C,B_C(F)\\) described in Proposition 10.</p><p>Every component has a ground countable cofinal chain.</p>", "proof": "<p>Successors of a vertex are directed: contract at the join of two contraction indices. Any two vertices in one component have a common successor. Along a finite path, combine a common successor of the initial part with the final edge, using directedness at the shared vertex. The same induction handles any finite set.</p><p>Compare any finite chain of fiber identifications at a common successor. Both composites there are the same map. Thus gluing the disjoint union of the \\(B_v(F)\\) is consistent and creates no new identification within one fiber. Each fiber maps bijectively onto the glued set \\(B_C(F)\\). All label fibers are literally equal to a ground set \\(L_C\\). Proposition 9 gives their cardinalities.</p><p>Choose in \\(V\\) a vertex \\((r,U)\\) and a nondecreasing cofinal sequence \\((n_i)_{i&lt;\\omega}\\) in \\(U\\), with \\(n_0=1\\), using Lemma 1.1. Let \\(v_i\\) be its contraction at \\(n_i\\).</p><p>This chain is cofinal in the whole component. Compare a vertex with \\(v_0\\) at a common successor, a contraction of \\(v_0\\) at some \\(n\\in U\\). Choose \\(i\\) with \\(n\\sqsubseteq n_i\\). Then \\(v_i\\) is a successor of that common successor and hence of the given vertex.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, together with the preceding normality argument", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Setting.", "statement": "<p>Setting.</p><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the cofinal chain \\((v_i)\\) of Lemma 10.2, with diagrams \\(r_{n_i}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The subgroup\n\\[\nK_C=\\bigcup_{i&lt;\\omega}\\operatorname{Fix}(D_{r_{n_i}})\n\\]\nis normal in \\(G_C\\), and \\(|G_C/K_C|\\leq\\aleph_0\\) in \\(V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Each fixer is a subgroup of \\(G_q\\), since \\(D_q\\subseteq D_{r_{n_i}}\\). It fixes a vertex, hence lies in \\(G_C\\). The ranges decrease, so the subgroups increase and their union is a subgroup.</p><p>For \\(\\sigma\\in G_C\\), the moved chain is cofinal. For every \\(i\\), some \\(j\\) satisfies\n\\[\nD_{r_{n_j}}\\subseteq D_{\\sigma r_{n_i}}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\sigma\\operatorname{Fix}(D_{r_{n_i}})\\sigma^{-1}\n=\\operatorname{Fix}(D_{\\sigma r_{n_i}})\n\\subseteq\\operatorname{Fix}(D_{r_{n_j}}).\n\\]\nApply the same argument to \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) to obtain normality.</p><p>For countability, choose the tower of Proposition 1 with countable initial submonoid \\(T_0\\) containing all \\(n_i\\) and all their right quotients. For \\(\\pi\\in G_C\\), define its alignments\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_\\pi=\n\\{(l,m)\\in\\omega\\times U:\\pi r_{n_l}=r_m\\}.\n\\]\nThis set is nonempty, because the moved chain is cofinal and one of its terms is a successor of \\(v_0\\). Each second coordinate divides some \\(n_N\\).</p><p>For \\((l,m)\\in\\mathcal A_\\pi\\) and any \\(N\\), choose \\(l'\\geq l\\) such that \\(\\pi v_{l'}\\) is a successor of \\(v_N\\). Its diagram is \\(r_k\\), where \\(k\\in U\\) and \\(n_N\\sqsubseteq k\\). Let \\(d\\in T_0\\) satisfy \\(n_l d=n_{l'}\\). Then\n\\[\nr_k=\\pi r_{n_{l'}}=(\\pi r_{n_l})_d=r_{md}.\n\\]\nInjectivity of \\(r\\) gives \\(k=md\\). Thus \\((l',md)\\) is an alignment, with \\(n_N\\sqsubseteq md\\).</p><p>Proposition 2 supplies an alignment \\((l,m)\\) with \\(m\\in T_0\\). There are countably many such pairs. Two permutations realizing the same pair agree pointwise on \\(D_{r_{n_l}}\\), so their quotient belongs to \\(K_C\\). Enumerate the pairs and assign each coset the first pair realized by a member. Distinct cosets receive distinct pairs, proving countability.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Theorem 6.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the symmetric model \\(W\\) of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">For the local construction, fix \\(\\dot E,(d_0,p),q\\) as in Proposition 9: \\((d_0,p)\\) symmetrically forces a nonwellorderable quotient, \\(D_p\\) supports \\(\\dot E\\) and contains the Cohen support, and \\(q_e=p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the components, glued fibers, transport maps, and groups \\(G_C,K_C\\) of Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In \\(W\\), every nonwellorderable surjective image of \\(A\\) is in bijection with \\(A\\).</p><p>For the fixed equivalence relation, the bijection \\(A\\to A/E\\) is represented below \\((d_0,q)\\) by one hereditarily symmetric graph with support \\(D_q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Full covariance on a component. For \\(\\gamma=\\pi K_C\\), define\n\\[\nf_C^\\gamma(F)(j)=\nT_\\pi^{\\pi^{-1}F}\n\\bigl(f_C^0(\\pi^{-1}F)(\\pi^{-1}j)\\bigr),\n\\qquad j\\in L_C.\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nReplacing \\(\\pi\\) by \\(\\pi k\\), \\(k\\in K_C\\), inserts \\(k^{-1}\\) in the source generic; (6.2) cancels its transport, and \\(k\\) fixes labels of \\(L_C\\). Thus this is independent of the representative.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nf_C(F)=f_C^{c_C(F)}(F).\n\\]\nEquations (6.4) and (6.6) give\n\\[\nf_C(\\sigma F)(\\sigma j)\n=T_\\sigma^F(f_C(F)(j))\n\\qquad(\\sigma\\in G_C).\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nThe preliminary computation is made at \\(\\pi^{-1}F\\); arbitrary transport of the computation at \\(F\\) would not give this identity.</p><p>Transport between components. Choose in \\(V\\) one component from each \\(G_q\\)-orbit, and make the preceding set-sized choices for those representatives. If \\(C'=\\sigma C\\), transport \\(f_C(\\sigma^{-1}F)\\) by \\(\\sigma\\). Different choices of \\(\\sigma\\) differ by an element of \\(G_C\\), so (6.1) gives the same assignment. Thus the assignments are covariant under \\(G_q\\).</p><p>Pulling back along \\(B_v(F)\\to B_C(F)\\) gives bijections\n\\[\nf_{r,U}(F):L_{r,U}\\longrightarrow B_{r,U}(F)\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]\ncommuting with every contraction and with transport.</p><p>Definability. Vertices, components, name codes, code wellorders, patterns, and auxiliary bijections are ground sets. Sections of product names can be constructed in an \\(\\mathbb R\\)-extension from its generic filter, and ordinary forcing for the ground set \\(\\mathbb P\\) is definable there. Symbolic quotients and gluing use equivalence relations on sets. Remaining operations are set operations, least elements of prescribed wellorders, and unique order isomorphisms.</p><p>Consequently an ordinary set-theoretic formula defines\n\\[\n\\Phi(F,r,j,\\tau):\n\\quad\n\\tau\\in\\mathcal X(r)\\text{ represents }\nf_{r,U_j}(F)(j)\\text{ in }D_F(r).\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nThe ideal \\(U_j\\) is the ground data of (4.3). A formal implementation uses a ground set of normalized name codes and its ordinary evaluation relation to form their sections. Ground codes and the forcing names they code are kept distinct; check names for codes are not falsely treated as moving to transported codes.</p><p>The construction gives\n\\[\n\\Phi(F,r,j,\\tau)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n\\Phi(\\pi F,\\pi r,\\pi j,\\pi\\tau)\n\\qquad(\\pi\\in G_q).\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\n\\dot F^\\pi=\\{(\\check{\\pi d},d):d\\in\\mathbb R\\},\n\\]\nwhose value at \\(F\\) is \\(\\pi F\\). The covariance proof gives, forced by \\(d_0\\), equivalence between\n\\[\n\\Phi(\\dot F,r,j,\\tau)\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\Phi(\\dot F^\\pi,\\pi r,\\pi j,\\pi\\tau),\n\\]\nwith all auxiliary ground choices fixed. Under the name action, \\(\\pi\\dot F^\\pi=\\dot F\\), while check names for the displayed ground arguments remain fixed. Ordinary forcing symmetry gives\n\\[\nd\\Vdash_{\\mathbb R}\\Phi(\\dot F,r,j,\\tau)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n\\pi d\\Vdash_{\\mathbb R}\\Phi(\\dot F,\\pi r,\\pi j,\\pi\\tau)\n\\qquad(d\\leq d_0).\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nThis is a ground relation for a fixed formula, not an argument requiring an externally transitive ground.</p><p>One symmetric graph. Using canonical equivariant ordered-pair names, form\n\\[\n\\dot J=\n\\left\\{\n\\bigl(\\langle\\dot A_j,\\tau\\rangle^\\bullet,(d,r)\\bigr):\n\\begin{array}{l}\nr\\leq q,\\ j\\in D_r,\\ \\tau\\in\\mathcal X(r),\\ d\\leq d_0,\\\\\nd\\Vdash_{\\mathbb R}\\Phi(\\dot F,r,j,\\tau)\n\\end{array}\n\\right\\}.\n\\tag{6.10}\n\\]\nAll indices range over ground sets. By (6.9), \\(G_q\\) permutes the entries, and applying \\(\\pi^{-1}\\) gives literal fixation. Every member name is a pair of \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) names. Thus \\(\\dot J\\) is \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\), supported by \\(D_q\\); member names need not share that support.</p><p>Take \\((F,H)\\ni(d_0,q)\\). For every \\(r\\in H\\) below \\(q\\), the graph contains exactly the local assignments (6.7): the Cohen truth lemma supplies active conditions for every true \\(\\Phi\\), and active entries force the asserted assignments.</p><p>If \\(t\\leq r\\leq q\\), \\(r=t_b\\), and \\(j\\in D_r\\), the availability ideal at \\(t\\) contains \\(b\\). Hence\n\\[\n(t,U_j\\text{ at }t)\\longrightarrow(r,U_j\\text{ at }r)\n\\]\nis a contraction, and (6.7) gives the same assigned symbolic element under inclusion. Their evaluations agree in every diagram generic containing \\(t\\).</p><p>Any two active entries occur below a common stronger diagram \\(t\\in H\\). At \\(t\\), exact availability ideals partition \\(D_t\\) and \\(D_F(t)\\), so the union of exact-fiber bijections is a bijection between those sets. Compatibility under strengthening proves single-valuedness and injectivity of \\(\\dot J^{F\\times H}\\). Local decision forces distinct symbolic elements unequal, and distinct label indices have distinct labels.</p><p>Every index belongs to a diagram in \\(H\\) below \\(q\\), by density of containing prescribed supports. Thus the graph has domain \\(A\\).</p><p>For any actual \\(E\\)-class, take one label in it and include its index in such a diagram \\(r\\). Its canonical class name represents an element of \\(D_F(r)\\), and the local fiber bijection reaches it. This is pointwise existence, not simultaneous selection of class representatives. The range is exactly \\(A/E\\), and the graph belongs to \\(W\\).</p><p>The construction works below a strengthening of every symmetric condition asserting the nonwellorderable-quotient hypothesis. The symmetric forcing theorem therefore proves the quotient assertion in \\(W\\).</p><p>Finally, for a surjection \\(h:A\\to Y\\) in \\(W\\), form its kernel relation \\(E\\). The map\n\\[\n[a]_E\\longmapsto h(a)\n\\]\nis a bijection \\(A/E\\to Y\\), defined by unique values without choosing representatives. If \\(Y\\) is nonwellorderable, compose it with the constructed bijection \\(A\\to A/E\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, including the preliminary-bijection construction of Section 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a component \\(C\\) and a cofinal chain with vertices \\(v_i\\) and diagrams \\(r_{n_i}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q_C\\) be the ground set of sequences\n  \\[\n  c=(\\tau_i)_{i&lt;\\omega},\\qquad \\tau_i\\in\\mathcal X(r_{n_i}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A code \\(c\\) represents \\(b\\in B_C(F)\\) if, from some index onward, \\(\\tau_i\\) represents an element of \\(B_{v_i}(F)\\) whose image in \\(B_C(F)\\) is \\(b\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a ground wellordering of \\(Q_C\\), of order type \\(\\lambda_C\\). Order \\(B_C(F)\\) by each element’s least representing code, and call this order \\(&lt;_F\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every element has a representing ground code. For \\(k\\in K_C\\), transport \\(T_k^F\\) is an order isomorphism\n\\[\n(B_C(F),&lt;_F)\\longrightarrow(B_C(kF),&lt;_{kF}),\n\\]\nand \\(K_C\\) fixes \\(L_C\\) pointwise.</p><p>Using fixed ground auxiliary bijections, there is a preliminary bijection\n\\[\nf_C^0(F):L_C\\longrightarrow B_C(F)\n\\]\nsatisfying\n\\[\nf_C^0(kF)(j)=T_k^F(f_C^0(F)(j))\n\\qquad(k\\in K_C,\\ j\\in L_C).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(b\\in B_C(F)\\), choose representatives at the chain vertices in \\(V[F]\\), using their bijections with \\(B_C(F)\\). This is a countable sequence of ground name codes, hence belongs to \\(V\\) by Proposition 3. Distinct elements have distinct least codes, so \\(&lt;_F\\) is a wellorder.</p><p>For \\(k\\in K_C\\), choose \\(N\\) such that \\(k\\) fixes \\(D_{r_{n_N}}\\) pointwise. For \\(i\\geq N\\), it fixes \\(r_{n_i}\\) and every name in \\(\\mathcal X(r_{n_i})\\) literally. Thus each particular ground code represents \\(b\\) at \\(F\\) if and only if the same code represents \\(T_k^F(b)\\) at \\(kF\\). Symmetry proves this on the tail, and the definition ignores the finite prefix. The sets of functioning codes and their minima are identical. Also \\(L_C\\subseteq D_{r_{n_i}}\\) for every \\(i\\), so \\(k\\) fixes every label in \\(L_C\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\theta_C(F)\\) be the order type of \\(&lt;_F\\). It is at most \\(\\lambda_C\\), and has cardinality \\(\\mu\\) in \\(V[F]\\). Preservation of \\(\\mu,\\mu^+\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mu\\leq\\theta_C(F)&lt;\\mu^+,\n\\qquad |\\theta_C(F)|^V=\\mu.\n\\]\nThe order type is a ground ordinal. These preserved endpoints justify the ground cardinality even if smaller cardinals collapse.</p><p>In \\(V\\), choose bijections \\(e_\\theta:\\mu\\to\\theta\\) for the set of \\(\\theta\\leq\\lambda_C\\) with ground cardinality \\(\\mu\\), and choose \\(l_C:\\mu\\to L_C\\). Let\n\\[\nb_F:\\theta_C(F)\\longrightarrow B_C(F)\n\\]\nbe the order isomorphism. Define\n\\[\nf_C^0(F)=b_F\\circ e_{\\theta_C(F)}\\circ l_C^{-1}.\n\\]\nThis is a bijection. The order covariance and pointwise fixation of \\(L_C\\) give (6.2).</p><p>The ground wellorder on all codes need not itself be invariant under \\(k\\); eventual fixation of each code’s entries is what is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(C\\), and put \\(\\Gamma_C=G_C/K_C\\), a countable ground group.</p><p class=\"li\">For each nonidentity coset \\(\\delta\\), choose a ground representative \\(\\gamma_\\delta\\) and points\n  \\[\n  s_{\\delta,i}\\in D_{r_{n_i}},\n  \\qquad\n  \\gamma_\\delta(s_{\\delta,i})\\neq s_{\\delta,i}\n  \\qquad(i&lt;\\omega).\n  \\]\n  Such points exist because otherwise \\(\\gamma_\\delta\\in K_C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an enumeration of the nonidentity cosets.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\alpha&lt;\\kappa\\) and \\(\\pi\\in G_C\\), define\n  \\[\n  P_F^\\alpha(\\pi K_C)=\n  \\left(\n  \\left[\n  (A_{\\pi(s_{\\delta,i})}^F\\upharpoonright\\alpha)_{i&lt;\\omega}\n  \\right]_{\\mathrm{ev}}\n  :\\delta\\in\\Gamma_C\\setminus\\{1\\}\n  \\right),\n  \\tag{6.3}\n  \\]\n  where \\([\\cdot]_{\\mathrm{ev}}\\) means equivalence modulo eventual equality, taken in the ground set of sequences of subsets of \\(\\alpha\\). Rows are viewed as binary characteristic functions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix ground wellorders of the possible patterns.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Formula (6.3) is independent of the representative of \\(\\pi K_C\\). For each \\(F\\), some \\(\\alpha&lt;\\kappa\\) distinguishes all cosets by their patterns. There is a definable selector \\(c_C(F)\\in\\Gamma_C\\) satisfying\n\\[\nc_C(\\sigma F)=\\sigma c_C(F)\n\\qquad(\\sigma\\in G_C).\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Every \\(\\alpha&lt;\\omega_1\\) is countable. Bounded row restrictions and the countably many coordinates for all detectors form a countable sequence of ground bits and indices. Proposition 3 puts these data in \\(V\\). Eventual equality of ground sequences is unchanged. Thus possible patterns, for all \\(\\alpha&lt;\\kappa\\), form a ground set.</p><p>If \\(k\\in K_C\\), then \\(k\\) fixes \\(s_{\\delta,i}\\) for all sufficiently large \\(i\\). Replacing \\(\\pi\\) by \\(\\pi k\\) changes only finitely many entries of each detector sequence. This proves representative independence.</p><p>If \\(\\pi K_C\\neq\\pi'K_C\\), use the detector\n\\[\n\\delta=(\\pi^{-1}\\pi')K_C,\n\\]\nand write \\(\\pi^{-1}\\pi'=\\gamma_\\delta k\\), \\(k\\in K_C\\). Eventually \\(k(s_{\\delta,i})=s_{\\delta,i}\\), and then\n\\[\n\\pi(s_{\\delta,i})\\neq\\pi'(s_{\\delta,i}).\n\\]\nDistinct indices give distinct Cohen rows. For every tail position choose a differing bit. Their countably many ordinals are bounded below the regular \\(\\kappa\\). Above that bound, the restricted sequences disagree at every tail position, so are inequivalent modulo eventual equality.</p><p>There are countably many pairs of cosets. A further countable supremum gives one bound distinguishing all pairs. These choices are in \\(V[F]\\), using regularity of \\(\\kappa\\), not closure of \\(\\mathbb P\\).</p><p>At the least distinguishing \\(\\alpha\\), choose the coset with least pattern in the fixed ground wellorder. If \\(\\Gamma_C\\) is trivial, choose its unique element and use \\(\\alpha=0\\).</p><p>For \\(\\sigma\\in G_C\\),\n\\[\nA_{\\sigma s}^{\\sigma F}=A_s^F,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nP_{\\sigma F}^\\alpha((\\sigma\\pi)K_C)\n=P_F^\\alpha(\\pi K_C).\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nThe pattern objects are literally equal ground objects. Left multiplication permutes the cosets, preserving the least distinguishing bound and the least pattern. This proves (6.4).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 7.1; distinctions from Remark 7.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.\nWork in \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\), and let \\(A\\neq\\varnothing\\) satisfy:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every nonempty set \\(X\\) admits a surjection \\(A\\times\\eta\\twoheadrightarrow X\\) for some ordinal \\(\\eta\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every ordinal-indexed family of nonempty sets has a choice function.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Every nonwellorderable surjective image of \\(A\\) is in bijection with \\(A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Partition Principle holds for all sets in this same model: every surjection \\(f:X\\twoheadrightarrow Y\\) has an injection \\(j:Y\\hookrightarrow X\\).</p><p>The third assumption is equivalently stated for quotients \\(A/E\\). The first assumption is the surjective statement \\(\\mathrm{SVC}(A)\\); no injective presentation \\(\\mathrm{SVC}^{+}(A)\\) is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Two observations in \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\). If \\(h:A\\twoheadrightarrow B\\) and \\(\\varnothing\\neq S\\subseteq B\\), choose one \\(s_0\\in S\\) and set\n\\[\nh_S(a)=\n\\begin{cases}\nh(a),&amp;h(a)\\in S,\\\\\ns_0,&amp;h(a)\\notin S.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThen \\(h_S:A\\twoheadrightarrow S\\). This chooses a default element only for one given subset, not simultaneously for a family.</p><p>Also, the image of a wellorderable set is wellorderable: use least preimages in a fixed wellordering.</p><p>Fix \\(f:X\\twoheadrightarrow Y\\). If \\(Y=\\varnothing\\), use the empty injection. Otherwise choose \\(s:A\\times\\eta\\twoheadrightarrow X\\), and for \\(i&lt;\\eta\\) define\n\\[\nB_i=s[A\\times\\{i\\}],\n\\]\n\\[\nY_i=f[B_i]\\setminus\\bigcup_{k&lt;i}f[B_k],\n\\]\n\\[\nS_i=B_i\\cap f^{-1}[Y_i].\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nSeparation and Replacement form these sequences. Each \\(y\\in Y\\) belongs to the unique \\(Y_i\\) corresponding to its least slice index. Thus the \\(Y_i\\) partition \\(Y\\). The \\(S_i\\) are pairwise disjoint and\n\\[\nf\\upharpoonright S_i:S_i\\twoheadrightarrow Y_i.\n\\]\nThey need not cover \\(X\\).</p><p>Before making choices, define\n\\[\nJ_i=\n\\{j\\in\\mathcal P(Y\\times X):\nj\\text{ is injective},\\\n\\operatorname{dom}(j)=Y_i,\\\n\\operatorname{ran}(j)\\subseteq S_i\\}.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nPower Set, Separation, and Replacement form \\(\\langle J_i:i&lt;\\eta\\rangle\\).</p><p>Fix \\(i\\). If \\(Y_i=\\varnothing\\), then \\(\\varnothing\\in J_i\\). If \\(Y_i\\) is nonempty and wellorderable, choose a wellordering of this one set. The nonempty fibers\n\\[\nS_i\\cap f^{-1}[\\{y\\}]\\qquad(y\\in Y_i)\n\\]\nare ordinal-indexed. Assumption 2 supplies a choice function, which is an injection \\(Y_i\\to S_i\\), so \\(J_i\\neq\\varnothing\\).</p><p>If \\(Y_i\\) is nonwellorderable, so is \\(S_i\\), since it surjects onto \\(Y_i\\). The slice \\(a\\mapsto s(a,i)\\) maps \\(A\\) onto \\(B_i\\). By the first observation, its nonempty subset \\(S_i\\) is an image of \\(A\\), and composition makes \\(Y_i\\) an image of \\(A\\). Assumption 3 makes both bijective to \\(A\\). Composing the two bijections gives an injection \\(Y_i\\to S_i\\), hence \\(J_i\\neq\\varnothing\\).</p><p>The pointwise proof has selected neither a sequence of wellorderings nor a sequence of quotient bijections. Apply assumption 2 to the already defined nonempty candidate sets \\(J_i\\). Obtain \\(j_i\\in J_i\\), and put\n\\[\nj=\\bigcup_{i&lt;\\eta}j_i.\n\\]\nDisjointness of \\(Y_i\\) makes this a function on \\(Y\\). If \\(j(y)=j(y')\\), disjointness of the corresponding \\(S_i\\) makes their indices equal, and injectivity of that \\(j_i\\) gives \\(y=y'\\).</p><p>For the quotient formulation, the kernel\n\\[\naE_h a'\\Longleftrightarrow h(a)=h(a')\n\\]\nof a surjection \\(h:A\\twoheadrightarrow B\\) gives the bijection\n\\[\n[a]_{E_h}\\longmapsto h(a).\n\\]\nUnique class values define its graph without representatives.</p><p>The injective presentation \\(\\mathrm{SVC}^{+}(A)\\) follows afterward by applying \\(\\mathrm{PP}\\) to each presentation. The proof does not replace assumption 3 by mere reverse injections for images of \\(A\\), nor by the different assertion concerning surjections between arbitrary subsets of \\(A\\). The resulting injection need not be a section of the original surjection.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Theorem 7.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\models\\mathrm{ZFC}\\) be a ground model.</p><p class=\"li\">In \\(V\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\kappa=\\omega_1^V,\\qquad\n  \\mu=((2^{\\aleph_0})^+)^V,\\qquad |I|^V=\\mu^+.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the monoid of Proposition 1 and symmetric system of Propositions 3–4.</p><p class=\"li\">For an arbitrary ground, read the construction in its internal forcing interpretation: ground objects are represented by check names, and \\(\\omega\\) and ordinals are internal. For an externally transitive ground, use ordinary evaluation and literal inclusions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The resulting symmetric model \\(W\\) satisfies\n\\[\nW\\models\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{PP}+\\mathrm{AC}_{\\mathrm{WO}}+\\neg\\mathrm{AC}.\n\\]\nIt contains the ground and has the same ordinals. If \\(b\\in V\\) and \\(z\\in W\\) is a function \\(\\omega\\to b\\), then \\(z\\in V\\).</p><p>The label set \\(A\\) is not wellorderable, and\n\\[\n\\mathcal P^W(A)\\setminus\\{\\varnothing\\}\n\\]\nis an explicit family in \\(W\\) having no choice function in \\(W\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 4 gives full pure-set \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\), the canonical ground embedding, and preservation of interpreted ordinals. For externally transitive grounds, its evaluated interpretation is transitive.</p><p>Proposition 6 gives the first assumption of Proposition 12 inside \\(W\\). Proposition 7 gives ordinal-indexed choice. Proposition 11 gives the classification of nonwellorderable images of \\(A\\). Proposition 12 therefore proves \\(\\mathrm{PP}\\) for every surjection in \\(W\\), with its injection in \\(W\\).</p><p>Proposition 8 proves that \\(A\\) is not wellorderable and that no countable sequence of ground elements is added. Lemma 13.1, applied inside \\(W\\), gives the displayed family without a choice function.</p><p>The construction is internal to choice grounds. The monoid is built by recursion along a set ordinal; its rearrangement uses an elementary submodel of a set structure \\(H_\\Theta\\). Its presentations, diagrams, permutations, and supports are sets. Local collections \\(\\mathcal X(r)\\) are ground sets. The fixed-formula predicate \\(\\Phi\\) and (6.10) form an actual ground \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) graph name.</p><p>Thus, in particular, \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}+V=L\\) proves that there is a nonempty set symmetric system \\(\\mathcal S\\) such that\n\\[\n1\\Vdash_{\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}}\n\\mathrm{PP}\\land\\neg\\mathrm{AC}\\land\\mathrm{AC}_{\\mathrm{WO}}.\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\nQuantifiers range over all internal \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) names, without a rank cutoff. Witnesses are produced below strengthenings of conditions asserting their hypotheses; one symmetric witness need not work below every condition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Work in \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(A\\) is not wellorderable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The family \\(\\mathcal P(A)\\setminus\\{\\varnothing\\}\\) has no choice function.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(c\\) is such a choice function. Let \\(\\lambda\\) be the Hartogs ordinal of \\(A\\), so no injection \\(\\lambda\\to A\\) exists. Foundation gives \\(A\\notin A\\).</p><p>By transfinite recursion define \\(a:\\lambda\\to A\\cup\\{A\\}\\). At stage \\(\\alpha\\), put\n\\[\nS_\\alpha=A\\setminus\\operatorname{ran}(a\\upharpoonright\\alpha),\n\\]\nand set\n\\[\na(\\alpha)=\n\\begin{cases}\nc(S_\\alpha),&amp;S_\\alpha\\neq\\varnothing,\\\\\nA,&amp;S_\\alpha=\\varnothing.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIf all \\(S_\\alpha\\) are nonempty, distinct stages give distinct members of \\(A\\), contradicting Hartogs. Hence there is a least \\(\\alpha&lt;\\lambda\\) with \\(S_\\alpha=\\varnothing\\). Before it, no value is the sentinel \\(A\\), so \\(a\\upharpoonright\\alpha\\) is a bijection onto \\(A\\), wellordering \\(A\\), a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Lemma 8.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">For a sentence or axiom instance \\(\\alpha\\), let \\(\\alpha^L\\) be its relativization to the constructible universe.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every axiom \\(\\alpha\\) of \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}+V=L\\), \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\) proves \\(\\alpha^L\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Con}(\\mathrm{ZF})\n\\Longrightarrow\\operatorname{Con}(\\mathrm{ZFC}+V=L).\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}+V=L\\) proves the generalized continuum hypothesis.</p><p>In this choice ground, the construction parameters specialize to\n\\[\n\\kappa=\\aleph_1,\\qquad\\mu=\\aleph_2,\\qquad I=\\aleph_3.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The constructible-universe theorem proves in \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\) the relativization to \\(L\\) of each \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\) axiom, Choice, and \\(V=L\\), including individual instances of Separation and Replacement, as cited in the paper at [17].</p><p>Relativization commutes with Boolean connectives and preserves first-order inference when quantifiers are restricted to a nonempty interpreted domain. A finite contradiction proof from \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}+V=L\\) would therefore translate into a contradiction proof from \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\), using only finitely many interpreted axiom instances. This proves the consistency implication.</p><p>The constructible-universe theorem proves GCH in \\(L\\), hence GCH from \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}+V=L\\). In that ground,\n\\[\n\\mu^{\\aleph_0}=(2^\\kappa)^{\\aleph_0}=\\mu,\n\\]\ngiving \\(\\mu^{&lt;\\kappa}=\\mu\\). CH also gives \\(\\kappa^{&lt;\\kappa}=\\kappa\\), but this extra equality is not assumed in the arbitrary-ground construction. A sufficiently large regular \\(\\Theta\\) suffices for the elementary-submodel argument; no inaccessible cardinal or universe satisfaction predicate is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemmas 8.2 and 8.3 and Section 8.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">A set symmetric system is a triple\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal S=(\\mathbb Q,\\Gamma,\\mathcal F),\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is a nonempty set poset with largest condition \\(1\\), \\(\\Gamma\\) is a set group of automorphisms, and \\(\\mathcal F\\) is a normal filter of subgroups.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}\\) be the class of hereditarily symmetric names.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M=(|M|,E^M)\\) be any countable, possibly externally ill-founded model of \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}\\), containing such a system.</p><p class=\"li\">The external poset is\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb Q^M=\\{p\\in|M|:M\\models p\\in\\mathbb Q\\},\n  \\]\n  with order interpreted by \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An \\(M\\)-generic filter meets every interpreted \\(D\\in|M|\\) that \\(M\\) regards as dense in \\(\\mathbb Q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an \\(M\\)-generic filter through any prescribed condition. For such a filter \\(G\\), the quotient of internal \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) names is an external set structure \\(W\\) satisfying the truth lemma for every standard first-order formula and satisfying full \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\).</p><p>If \\(M\\) asserts \\(1\\Vdash_{\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}}\\psi\\) for a standard sentence \\(\\psi\\), then \\(W\\models\\psi\\). No external recursion on \\(E^M\\), and no external well-foundedness of \\(M\\), is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Externally enumerate the dense-set codes in the countable model \\(M\\). Starting through a prescribed condition, choose a descending sequence meeting them successively. Internal density supplies an actual strengthening in the external poset, since its witnesses are domain elements of \\(M\\). The upward closure is \\(M\\)-generic. Neither closure of the forcing nor well-foundedness of \\(E^M\\) is needed.</p><p>The quotient and its symmetric substructure are external set structures because \\(|M|\\) is a set. Lemma 15.2 gives their symmetric truth lemma and full \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\), using Lemma 15.1 for every standard axiom instance. The same truth lemma transfers any standard sentence symmetrically forced by \\(1\\) in \\(M\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Work in \\(\\mathrm{ZFC}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal S=(\\mathbb Q,\\Gamma,\\mathcal F)\\) is a set symmetric system as in Proposition 15.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each fixed formula \\(\\varphi\\), the relation\n\\[\np\\Vdash_{\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}}\\varphi(\\tau_1,\\ldots,\\tau_n),\n\\qquad \\tau_i\\in\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S},\n\\]\nis definable. It satisfies the first-order forcing rules with equality and the symmetry lemma.</p><p>For every individual axiom \\(\\alpha\\) of \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\), including every Separation and Replacement instance,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{ZFC}\\vdash\n\\bigl(\\mathcal S\\text{ is a set symmetric system}\n\\Longrightarrow\n1\\Vdash_{\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}}\\alpha\\bigr).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Define atomic forcing by the usual recursion on names. For a fixed formula, extend through its finitely many connectives and quantifiers, restricting quantified names to \\(\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}\\).</p><p>For example, let\n\\[\nD_{\\varphi,\\vec\\tau}=\n\\{q\\in\\mathbb Q:\n(\\exists\\sigma\\in\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S})\\\nq\\Vdash_{\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}}\\varphi(\\sigma,\\vec\\tau)\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\np\\Vdash_{\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}}\\exists x\\,\\varphi(x,\\vec\\tau)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nD_{\\varphi,\\vec\\tau}\\text{ is dense below }p.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nThis is a set by Separation from \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Names and hereditary symmetry are definable by set recursion. Thus these are ordinary first-order predicates, as a schema indexed externally by formulas, not one satisfaction predicate for all formulas.</p><p>Logical and symmetry rules follow by induction on a fixed formula, with the internal name recursion handling atomic formulas. The paper invokes the standard symmetric-model theorem at [10, 11] and recalls the following all-rank constructions.</p><p>Separation. For a formula \\(\\varphi(x,\\vec a)\\), domain name \\(\\sigma\\), and parameter names \\(\\vec\\tau\\), use pairs\n\\[\n(\\rho,q)\\in\\operatorname{dom}(\\sigma)\\times\\mathbb Q\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nq\\Vdash_{\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}}\n\\rho\\in\\sigma\\land\\varphi(\\rho,\\vec\\tau).\n\\]\nThe intersection of the stabilizers of \\(\\sigma\\) and the finitely many parameters fixes this name and belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\). Member names are hereditarily symmetric. Atomic forcing and (8.2) verify the desired subset, with no formula or rank restriction.</p><p>Replacement. Define\n\\[\n\\operatorname{nrk}(\\eta)=\n\\sup\\{\\operatorname{nrk}(\\zeta)+1:(\\zeta,r)\\in\\eta\\}.\n\\]\nFor a fixed relation \\(\\psi(x,y,\\vec a)\\), domain name \\(\\sigma\\), and parameters \\(\\vec\\tau\\), consider each pair\n\\[\n(\\rho,q)\\in\\operatorname{dom}(\\sigma)\\times\\mathbb Q\n\\]\nfor which some \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name \\(\\eta\\) satisfies\n\\[\nq\\Vdash_{\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}}\\psi(\\rho,\\eta,\\vec\\tau).\n\\]\nTake the least possible name-rank of such \\(\\eta\\). Ground Replacement bounds these ranks strictly below one ordinal \\(\\beta\\).</p><p>All names of rank \\(&lt;\\beta\\) form a set, so\n\\[\n\\dot B_\\beta=\n\\{(\\eta,1):\\eta\\in\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S},\\\n\\operatorname{nrk}(\\eta)&lt;\\beta\\}\n\\]\nis a set name. Automorphisms preserve name-rank and hereditary symmetry, making it invariant and hereditarily symmetric.</p><p>Below a condition forcing totality on \\(\\sigma\\), local witnesses from (8.2) can be replaced at the same witnessing conditions by names in \\(\\dot B_\\beta\\). This supplies a set bounding the witnesses. For a functional relation, Separation gives its image. The bound depends on the particular formula and domain, not on the universe of names.</p><p>Power Set. Normalize a name for a subset of \\(\\sigma\\) using member names from \\(\\operatorname{dom}(\\sigma)\\) and membership forcing tests. Normalized names lie in\n\\[\n\\mathcal P(\\operatorname{dom}(\\sigma)\\times\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nUse all hereditarily symmetric candidates under conditions forcing them to be subsets of \\(\\sigma\\). The stabilizer of \\(\\sigma\\) permutes this collection, giving an \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) power-set name. Every symmetric subset has such a normalization.</p><p>Check names, canonical pairing, and union supply the remaining elementary existence axioms. The internal name-rank argument gives Foundation. Equivalently, the interpreted hereditary class is membership-closed inside the ordinary extension; elements of its objects have active \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) member names. Extensionality and Foundation inherit witnesses from the ordinary extension. This is first-order Foundation, not external well-foundedness.</p><p>These constructions are ground-theory proofs for each chosen formula. They use local existential witnesses, not a symmetric-forcing maximum principle, which need not hold.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M,\\mathcal S\\) be as in Proposition 15, and let \\(G\\) be \\(M\\)-generic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N^M\\) be the external set of elements that \\(M\\) regards as \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-names.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\sigma\\sim_G\\tau\n  \\Longleftrightarrow\n  (\\exists p\\in G)\\ M\\models p\\Vdash\\sigma=\\tau,\n  \\tag{8.3}\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  [\\sigma]_G\\,E_G\\,[\\tau]_G\n  \\Longleftrightarrow\n  (\\exists p\\in G)\\ M\\models p\\Vdash\\sigma\\in\\tau.\n  \\tag{8.4}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M[G]\\) be the quotient structure of all internal names, and let \\(W\\) be its substructure of classes with an internally hereditarily symmetric representative.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every standard formula \\(\\varphi\\) and internal \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) names \\(\\vec\\tau\\),\n\\[\nW\\models\\varphi([\\vec\\tau]_G)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n(\\exists p\\in G)\\\nM\\models p\\Vdash_{\\mathrm{HS}_{\\mathcal S}}\\varphi(\\vec\\tau).\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(W\\models\\mathrm{ZF}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Internal equality and substitution rules, together with directedness of \\(G\\), make \\(\\sim_G\\) an equivalence relation and \\(E_G\\) independent of representatives.</p><p>Induct externally on the finite syntax of the standard formula, using atomic formulas, conjunction, negation, and existential quantification.</p><p>The atomic case is (8.3)–(8.4), since atomic symmetric forcing agrees with ordinary forcing. Conjunction uses common strengthenings and monotonicity.</p><p>For negation, the set deciding the particular formula is an internally definable dense subset of \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Separation in \\(M\\) gives its code, and genericity gives a deciding condition in \\(G\\). The induction hypothesis excludes the wrong decision; directedness excludes opposite forced answers.</p><p>If \\(p\\in G\\) symmetrically forces an existential statement, the local witness conditions in (8.2), together with conditions incompatible with \\(p\\), form an internally definable dense set. Genericity meets its first part. Its internal witness is an actual element of \\(|M|\\) that \\(M\\) regards as an \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) name. Its equivalence class is a witness in \\(W\\) by induction.</p><p>Conversely, a witness in \\(W\\) has an internal \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\) representative. Apply the induction hypothesis and existential forcing introduction.</p><p>This proves truth for every standard formula. Lemma 15.1, applied inside \\(M\\) to each standard \\(\\mathrm{ZF}\\) axiom instance, gives \\(W\\models\\mathrm{ZF}\\).</p><p>No name is evaluated by external recursion over \\(E^M\\), and no truth predicate for all, possibly nonstandard, formula codes is used.</p><p>Membership closure is also direct. If \\([\\sigma]_G E_G[\\tau]_G\\) with \\(\\tau\\) internally \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\), atomic forcing and genericity give an active member name \\(\\rho\\) of \\(\\tau\\) with \\([\\rho]_G=[\\sigma]_G\\). Heredity makes \\(\\rho\\) internally \\(\\mathrm{HS}\\). Thus \\(W\\) and \\(M[G]\\) have the same members of every \\(W\\)-object. This relational transitivity does not make \\(E_G\\) externally well founded.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [3, 10, "cite"], [3, 11, "cite"], [3, 13, "cite"], [3, 19, "cite"], [3, 23, "cite"], [3, 29, "cite"], [3, 34, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 25, "cite"], [4, 28, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 3, "cite"], [8, 3, "cite"], [9, 3, "cite"], [10, 29, "cite"], [11, 14, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [11, 17, "cite"], [11, 18, "cite"], [11, 22, "cite"], [11, 23, "cite"], [11, 25, "cite"], [11, 31, "cite"], [11, 32, "cite"], [11, 34, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [14, 1, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [14, 19, "cite"], [14, 20, "cite"], [14, 23, "cite"], [14, 30, "cite"], [14, 34, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [15, 22, "cite"], [15, 23, "cite"], [15, 24, "cite"], [15, 25, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 34, "cite"], [19, 34, "cite"], [20, 34, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [22, 20, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [23, 28, "cite"], [23, 30, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [26, 30, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [28, 26, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [29, 26, "cite"], [30, 34, "cite"], [31, 30, "part"], [32, 30, "part"], [33, 34, "cite"], [34, 0, "cite"], [34, 1, "cite"], [35, 34, "cite"], [36, 0, "cite"], [37, 0, "cite"], [38, 0, "cite"], [38, 37, "cite"], [38, 39, "cite"], [39, 37, "cite"]]});
